astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_000,0,"comparedto the classical method most appropriate for that regime. Nevertheless, its clear advantageisto span ina single formulation both the Laplace and Legendre expansions. It may thereforebe useful when the system transitions between diﬀerent dynamical regimes, or when the prevailing regime cannotbe determineda priori. Overall, the Laplace–Legendre expansion provides consistent per formance acrossa wide rangeof orbital parameters which includes the domainof applic abilityof both the Laplace and Legendre expansions, makingitan interesting option for secular stud ies. The priceto pay for this broader applicabilityisan increasing comp lexityof the analytical expressionof the Hamiltonianat each order with respectto the classical expansions, which results ina largernumber ofterms involvedata given order.In par ticular, the expressionof the secular Hamiltonian involvesan infinites ummation over the Laplace coeﬃcients and thereforeis not ina closed form. Thisna turally calls for the use ofa computer algebra systemto manipulate large expressions, particularly for the spatial problem, whichwe did not addressin this exploratory work. Extending the Laplace–Legendreformulationtothe spatialcaseis feasiblealongthe samelines ofthis work, but would substantially increase the algebraic complexity, asis already appar entin the classical expansions when moving from the planarto thesp atial formulation","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function",,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_001,1,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function for the planar planetary 3-body problem. This formulation, referredtoas the Laplace–Legendre expansion, combines elements from both the cla ssical Laplace and Legendreseries.ItusesthesameseriesstructureastheLaplac eformulation,but diﬀersin that each termof the expansion retains its full dependencein both the semi-major axis ratio and eccentricity. The method provides, for example, ana lternative meansto construct the secular Hamiltonian suitable for modeling the long-t erm evolutionof planetary systems. We evaluated the convergenceof this expansion across severalc hoicesof eccen tricities eand semi-major axis ratios α, and compared its performance with thatof the Laplace and Legendre series. We explored configurationsch aracterized bye,α∈[0,0.2], as wellas two limiting regimes: nearly circular, compact systems withe= 0.01andα∈ {0.4,0.5},andhierarchical,highlyeccentricsystemswithe= 0.6and α∈ {0.001,0.01}. The results show that the Laplace–Legendre expansion performs bestin regimes where both eandαare small (e.g., e= 0.01,α= 0.01). It can also outperform both classical methodsinan intermediate regime, where neither param eteris particularly small but their product remains small (e.g., e= 0.1,α= 0.1). In the hierarchical limit ( α <0.1), the Laplace–Legendre expansion improves","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function",,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,4.5
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_002,2,"small (e.g., e= 0.01,α= 0.01). It can also outperform both classical methodsinan intermediate regime, where neither param eteris particularly small but their product remains small (e.g., e= 0.1,α= 0.1). In the hierarchical limit ( α <0.1), the Laplace–Legendre expansion improves upon the Legendre expansionat low eccentricities ( e <0.1), whileat higher eccentricities (e≥0.2) it closely reproduces the performanceof the Legendre expans ion. In the nearly circular regime ( e/lessorsimilar0.01), andas longas αremains moderate, its behavior closely matches thatof the Laplace expansion. In the last two regimes, combining the Laplace and Legendre approaches does not necessarily leadtoan expansion that outperforms both classical methodsat the same time. Instead, the Laplace–Legendre expansion tendsto match the method thatis best suitedto each limit. In the extremesof these limits (namely, when α≪1 andeis large, or whene∼0 whileαis large), the new expansion may exhibit slightly reduced accuracy comparedto the classical method most appropriate for that regime. Nevertheless, its clear advantageisto span ina single formulation both the Laplace and Legendre expansions. It may thereforebe useful when the system transitions between diﬀerent dynamical regimes, or when the prevailing regime","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function",,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,4.5
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_003,3,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian Abstract We explorea hybrid expansionof the disturbing function inp lanetary dynamics that combines elementsof the classical Laplace and Legendre developments. This formulation retains the structureof the Laplace expansion, but expresses the inverseof the mutual distance asa series whose terms keepan exact dependenceon both the eccentricity and the semi-major axis ratio. We useitto construct the first-order secular Hamiltonianof the planar 3-body pro blem, relevant for modeling the long-term evolutionof planetary systems. Wea ssess the conver genceof the new expansion numerically and compareit withthatof the Laplace and Legendre series acrossa rangeof orbital configurations. The results show thatthenew expansionprovidesconsistent performanceacr oss diversedynamical regimes, bridging the domainsof applicabilityof the twocl assical approaches. Keywords: Celestial mechanics, planetary dynamics, disturbing function, computer algebra",Abstract We explorea hybrid expansionof the disturbing function inp lanetary dynamics,Abstract We explorea hybrid expansionof the disturbing function inp lanetary dynamics,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,162,124,False,False,0,124,True,4.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_004,4,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 1 Introduction In planetary systems, the disturbing function playsa central rolein understanding the long-term dynamical evolutionof planetary orbits. It encodes the gravitational interactions between planets and provides the foundation for stu dying both secular and resonant dynamics. Expanding the disturbing function asa power seriesin the orbital elements has long beena key focusin celestial mechanics and fundamentalto constructing perturbative modelsof planetary motion. In the con textof the 3-body problem, two classical approaches are commonly used for this purp ose. The first approach expands the disturbing function asa power ser iesin eccen tricity and inclination, with coeﬃcients that are exactin semi-majora xes ratio and are expressedin termsof Laplace coeﬃcients. This formulation, de velopedby Laplace (1773) and Lagrange ( 1781;1782), forms the basisof whatis now knownas the Laplace–Lagrange secular theory (see Laskar 2013, fora detailed historical account). This theory has beena cornerstone for studying the long-termev olutionof planetary systems,particularlyinthesolarsystem,whereorbitsarenearlyc ircularandcoplanar. Peirce(1849) and Le Verrier( 1855)extended the expansionto higher degreesin eccen tricities and inclinations, aiming fora more accurate descriptionof the solar system’s evolution, followedby further similar developments",1 Introduction,1 Introduction,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_005,5,"account). This theory has beena cornerstone for studying the long-termev olutionof planetary systems,particularlyinthesolarsystem,whereorbitsarenearlyc ircularandcoplanar. Peirce(1849) and Le Verrier( 1855)extended the expansionto higher degreesin eccen tricities and inclinations, aiming fora more accurate descriptionof the solar system’s evolution, followedby further similar developments ( Boquet 1889;Newcomb 1895; Brouwer and Clemence 1961;Murray and Dermott 1999 ). This expansion was later reformulated withina Hamiltonian framework using heliocentric canon ical variables introducedby Poincar´ e ( Poincar´ e 1896;Laskar 1991;Laskar and Robutel 1995 ). The second approach develops the disturbing functionin powersof the semi-major axes ratioof the two planets, using Legendre polynomials. This expa nsion, knownas Legendre expansion, has been widely applied toa varietyof triple sys tems with hierar chicalconfigurations( Hansen 1852;Hill 1875;Tisserand1894;Kaula1962;Kozai1962; Lidov 1962;Brumberg 1967;Fordetal. 2000;Lee and Peale 2003;Laskar and Bou´ e 2010;Naozetal.2011 ),andis applicableto configurationswith arbitraryeccentricities and inclinations. Each expansion oﬀers advantagesin diﬀerent dynamical regimes. L aplace expan sionis well suitedto compact, nearly circular and coplanar orbits, asin the solar system, but has limitations when appliedto configurations with higher eccentricities and inclinations, suchas those observedin certain exoplanetarysy stems. In contrast, Legendre expansionis applicable for objects",1 Introduction,1 Introduction,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,4.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_006,6,"L aplace expan sionis well suitedto compact, nearly circular and coplanar orbits, asin the solar system, but has limitations when appliedto configurations with higher eccentricities and inclinations, suchas those observedin certain exoplanetarysy stems. In contrast, Legendre expansionis applicable for objects with arbitrary eccent ricities and inclina tions, but converges slowly when the systemis not strongly hierarc hical, asin the caseof the inner solar system ( Bou´ eetal. 2012 ). These complementary limitations natu rally raise the questionof whetheran alternative formulation canbe constructedto combine both approaches, and whether sucha formulation could lev erage the advan tagesof both. Ellis and Murray (2000) presenteda mixed expansionof the disturbing function “ which retains the advantagesof Kaula(1962)’s approach but expressedin termsof Laplace coeﬃcients and their derivatives ”. The final formof suchan expan sionis givenin their Eq. (16), but its detailed derivation was deferred toa subsequent development was only used asa basis ofa “ for producing expansionsof the planetaryof its convergence was performed. In this work, we explorean alternative developmentof the disturbing function, whichwe refertoas the Laplace–Legendre expansion. It retainsthe classical structureof the Laplace expansion, developing",1 Introduction,1 Introduction,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,4.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_007,7,"development was only used asa basis ofa “ for producing expansionsof the planetaryof its convergence was performed. In this work, we explorean alternative developmentof the disturbing function, whichwe refertoas the Laplace–Legendre expansion. It retainsthe classical structureof the Laplace expansion, developing the inverseof the mutual dist ance asa Taylor series around the circular, coplanar problem. The key diﬀerence ist hat, at each order, the termsof the new expansion are exactin both eccentricity andthe semi-major axis ratio. We present the new formulation for the planar 3-body problem, witha specific focuson its secular part, whichis relevant for modeling the long-ter morbital evolutionof planetary systems. We beginby outlining the Hamiltonian formalism for the planetary 3-body prob lem and reviewing the two classical approaches for expanding the dir ect partof the disturbing function (Sect. 2). We then introduce the Laplace–Legendre expansionat zeroth, first, and arbitrary order (Sect. 3). Finally, we assess its convergence proper ties,comparingitsaccuracywith thatoftheclassicalLaplaceandL egendreexpansions acrossa rangeof eccentricities and semi-major axis ratios (Sect. 4).",1 Introduction,1 Introduction,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,210,161,False,False,420,581,True,4.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_008,8,"the spatial problem, whichwe did not addressin this exploratory work. Extending the Laplace–Legendreformulationtothe spatialcaseis feasiblealongthe samelines ofthis work, but would substantially increase the algebraic complexity, asis already appar entin the classical expansions when moving from the planarto thesp atial formulation (e.g.,Laskar and Bou´ e 2010 ). Whilea dedicated study wouldbe requiredto quantify convergencein the spatial case, the new formulation remainsa for mal expansionin the parameterV. We therefore expect similar qualitative convergence behaviorin the regime where Vremains small, i.e., for smallto moderate eccentricities, inclinations and semi-major axis ratios. AppendixA Laplace-Legendre expansionof order 2 We present here the Laplace–Legendre expansionof order 2, defi nedby a2","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function","5 Conclusion In this work, we exploredan alternative expansionof the disturbing function",,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,134,103,False,False,420,523,True,4.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_009,9,"Fig. 3couldbe justifiedby the fact that the new formulation orders the seriesin powersof the parameterV, rather thanin the small parameter thatis appropriateto each reg ime (efor Laplace, αfor Legendre). Asa result, in these limiting cases the orderingis notas locally opti malasin the corresponding classical expansion. This isa trade-oﬀ for the optimalityof the new expansionin the regimee,α→0. Anyway, the clear advantageof the new expansionis that nowwe havea global formulation that bridges theL aplace and Leg endre regimes.This broaderapplicability comesat the cost ofa large rnumber ofterms retained ata given expansion order comparedto the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions (see AppendixB). Finally, we note that when both eandαare not small (e.g, e,α >0.2), the convergenceoftheLaplace-Legendreexpansiondeterioratesa ndbecomesprogressively slower, similarlyto the classical expansions. This behavioris expected, asin such regimestheparameter Visnolongersmallandtheassumptionsunderlyingthepresent",4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,163,125,False,False,1400,1525,False,3.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_010,10,"part T1, arising from the non-inertial natureof the heliocentric frame, anda direct part U1, describing the mutual gravitational interaction between the plan ets. The indirect termT1averagesto zero over the fast orbital angles Miand thus does not contributeto the averaged Hamiltonianin secular theories limitedto first-orderin planetary masses. We therefore focusin this workon the expansionof the dir ect partU1, which encapsulates the main complexityof the perturbing function andgo verns the secular dynamicsof planetary systems. We focus primarilyon the planar pro blem and beginby summarizing the two classical methods usedto expand this term.",2 Hamiltonian formulation and expansionof the disturbing function,2 Hamiltonian formulation and expansionof the disturbing function,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,117,90,False,False,140,230,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_011,11,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian 4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion The convergenceof truncated secular expansionsof the disturb ing function has been investigatedin several previous studies, primarily through numerical assessmentsof theaccuracyofhigh-orderexpansions.In( Laskar1985 ),theconvergenceoftheLaplace expansionat second orderof the massesis analyzed for all planetary pairsin the Solar System with respectto the truncationin the harmonicsof the first order expansion. For the coplanar 3-body problem, Libert & Henrard ( 2005) considered Hamiltonian expansions carriedto degree 12 in eccentricity and showed that the resulting secular models accurately reproduce the long-term dynamicsof exoplanet ary systems with large eccentricities, while also demonstrating that the seriesof sec ular terms con verges better than the series associated with the full perturbat ion. Migaszewski & Go´ zdziewski ( 2008) performeda similar convergencestudyof the Legendre expansion, extendingitupto order 24 in the semi-major axis ratio and applying ittoa rangeof exoplanetary configurations. They showed that the expansion converges rapidly for suﬃciently hierarchical systems, while the convergence becomesp rogressively sloweras the system becomes more compact. In this section, we compare the convergence propertiesof the La place–Legendre expansion introducedin Sect. 3with thoseof the classical",4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_012,12,"They showed that the expansion converges rapidly for suﬃciently hierarchical systems, while the convergence becomesp rogressively sloweras the system becomes more compact. In this section, we compare the convergence propertiesof the La place–Legendre expansion introducedin Sect. 3with thoseof the classical Laplace and Legendre expansions. In line with the aforementioned studies, we focus here ona numerical assessmentof convergence basedon the evaluationof the seriesat diﬀerent truncation orders.InthecelebratedchapterVIIIonFormalcalculus,in LesM´ ethodesNouvellesde laM´ ecaniqueC´ eleste, Poincar´ e ( 1892) distinguishes the convergencein the sensof the astronomers and mathematicians, and reminds that both approac hes are legitimate. As discussedin Sect. 2.2, we know that for suﬃciently small valuesof the ratioof semi major axis, α, eccentricities and inclinations, the series expansion ( 2.2) is convergent. Butin the present work, we consider formal expansions and address the convergence questions ina numerical way,in the sense ofthe astronomers,with out addressingmore mathematical questions suchas the domainof convergenceof the series, the sizeof the remainder, or the decay rateof the terms. Our goalisto assessthe accuracyof each seriesin approximating the secular direct partof the perturbingfu nction,/an}bracketle{tU1/an}bracketri}ht, asa functionof the truncation order. Asin the previous",4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,4 Convergenceof the Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,2.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_013,13,"Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian Laplace-Legendre expansionof the planar planetary Hamiltonian Aya Alnajjarine1*, Jacques Laskar1*and Federico Mogavero2,1 1LTE, CNRS, Observatoirede Paris, Universit´ e PSL, Sorbonne Universit´ e, 77 Avenue Denfert-Rochereau, 75014 Paris, Fr ance. 2Dipartimentodi Matematica “Tullio Levi-Civita”, Universit` a degli Studidi Padova, Via Trieste 63, 35121 Padova, Italy. *Corresponding author(s). E-mail(s): aya.alnajjarine@obspm.fr; jacques.laskar@obspm.fr; Contributing authors: mogavero@math.unipd.it;",Laplace-Legendre expansionof the planar planetary Hamiltonian,Laplace-Legendre expansionof the planar planetary Hamiltonian,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,77,59,False,False,0,59,False,1.0
astrobench_cc18f51e25,astrobench_cc18f51e25_chunk_014,14,"classical developmentsin regimes where botheandαare small, orin intermediate regimes where neither parameteris particula rly small but their product remains small. These regimes define the natural domainof interestof the present formulation, whichis explored quantitativelyin Sect. 4.Moreover, exact dependenceon eandαat each order should allowusto span the two regimese→0 andα→0inasingleformulation.Inthefollowing,weconstructtheLaplace–L egendre expansionat zeroth, first, and arbitrary order, focusingin each caseon the expressionof the secular direct partof the disturbing function.",3 Laplace-Legendre expansion,3 Laplace-Legendre expansion,,,,,Laplace-Legendre expansion of the planar planetary Hamiltonian,"Aya Alnajjarine, Jacques Laskar, Federico Mogavero",astro-ph.EP,2026-03-17T13:51:12Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16528v1,2603.16528,action_angle_methods,86,66,False,False,140,206,False,1.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_000,0,"Rein & D. Tamayo 2019). The jump step dependson Mercury’s momentum (and thatof the other planets) relativeto the Solar System barycenter. The Keplerian motion solved forin the Ke pler stepis also different from Jacobi case. Mercury does not orbit the Mercury-Sun barycenterasin the Jacobicase, but rather the Solar System barycenter5. In the limitof high eccentricity, the difference between Mer cury’s true trajectory and the Keplerian orbit the Ke pler solver solves for becomes significant especially near pericenter. The error ofa symplectic integrator (or more gener allyan operator splitting method) canbe understoodin termsofan infinite sumof commutators consistingof compositionsof the integrator’s individual steps (seee.g. P. Saha & S. Tremaine 1992; H. Reinetal. 2019; D. Tamayoetal. 2020). The commutators involving the jump step leadto error terms not presentin the caseof Jacobi coordinates anditis those commutators which leadto the artificial numerical precession. The comb like structure seenin the curve for DHC in Fig. 1 is dueto strong timestep resonances (K. P. Rauch & M. Hol man 1999). To suppress these timestep resonances and keep the artificial numerical precession rate below thatof GR we see",3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_001,1,"tric velocities. SeeM. J. Duncanetal. (1998); H. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). the eccentricityof planetsis high. The Solar Systemis one example where high accuracyis requiredto measure the correct instability rate because the eccentricityof Mercury becomes large (≳0.4) just before instabilities occur and numerical errors can suppress the behaviour. Reducing the timestepin simulations using DHC is one possible solution. Another, pursued byH. F. Lev ison & M. J. Duncan (2000) andT. Luetal. (2024) isto shift the problematic termsin the jump step during close pericenter passages into the Keplerian step andin tegrateit using Bulirsch-Stoer (J. E. Chambers 1999). However, we note thata simple scheme using Jacobico ordinates can reproduce the correct instability rate for surprisingly large timesteps (H. Reinetal. 2025). Asa result, statistically converged simulations with DHC are significantly more computationally expensive than simulations using Jacobi coordinatesso that Jacobico ordinates are advantageousin most scenarios. We have focused our experiments and discussionon the Solar System. However, the general advice for run ning simulationsof any planetary system where one wantsto integrate untilan instability occursis the same:Jacobi coordinates perform significantly better. As soonas orbit crossing",4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_002,2,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea long history (seee.g. W. J. Eckertetal. 1951 for oneof the first simulationsof this kind, andJ. Laskar 2013 fora historical summaryof the field). Although dif ferent authors use different numerical algorithms, most modern long-term simulations use symplectic integra tors that make useof the Wisdom-Holman splitting (J. L. Wisdom 1981; J. Wisdom & M. Holman 1991; H. Kinoshitaetal. 1991). Various variationsof the Wisdom-Holman (WH) algorithm exist, differingin the orderof the scheme, the coordinate system used for the Hamiltonian splitting (D. M. Hernandez & W. Dehnen 2017), and other details suchas whetheror not symplec tic correctors are used (fora comparisonsof methods seeH. Reinetal. 2019). Several authors have argued thatitis important for the adopted timesteptobe short enoughto resolve peri center passages, which becomes challengingat highor bital eccentricities like those achieved when Mercury goes unstable (K. P. Rauch & M. Holman 1999; J. Wis dom 2015; D. M. Hernandezetal. 2022). This paper shows that this criterion dependson the coordinate system used. To clarify,","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_003,3,"becomes challengingat highor bital eccentricities like those achieved when Mercury goes unstable (K. P. Rauch & M. Holman 1999; J. Wis dom 2015; D. M. Hernandezetal. 2022). This paper shows that this criterion dependson the coordinate system used. To clarify, wedo not referto thecoordinate systemin which the user inputs and outputs their positions and velocities (e.g. eclipticvs equato rial). Our focusis ratheron the choiceof coordinates usedto split offa dominant Keplerian term from the full Hamiltonianas partof the WH algorithm. The two most commonly used coordinate systems for the Hamil tonian splitting are Jacobi coordinates and democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC). Both coordinate sys tems have advantages and disadvantages. For example, DHC (M. J. Duncanetal. 1998) canbe used forhy brid symplectic integrators and canbe more easily par allelized (P. Javaherietal. 2023). Jacobi coordinateson the other hand are more accurate and canbe used with symplectic correctorsto achieve very high order (J. Wisdometal. 1996; S. Blanesetal. 2013). Fora pre cise definition anda more detailed comparisonof these coordinate systems seeH. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). Both coordinate systems have been usedto study the long-term evolution","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_004,4,"with symplectic correctorsto achieve very high order (J. Wisdometal. 1996; S. Blanesetal. 2013). Fora pre cise definition anda more detailed comparisonof these coordinate systems seeH. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). Both coordinate systems have been usedto study the long-term evolution and stabilityof the Solar System. The first direct integrations over 5 Gyr were performed byJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) with the SABA integrator, a high order integrator using Jacobi coordi nates (J. Laskar & P. Robutel 2001). More recently, N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) used the MER DHC to study the influenceof passing field starson the Solar System. Notably, the simulations that constitute the control group ofN. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) and shouldbe comparableto those ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) showan instability rateof only 0.1%, whereas the simulations ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) and others (R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbotetal. 2021; D. S. Abbotetal. 2023) resultinan instability ratean orderof magnitude larger,∼1%. Similar discrepancies were also observed byR. E. Zeebe (2015) who studied the position errorof Mer cury when using","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_005,5,"Gastineau (2009) and others (R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbotetal. 2021; D. S. Abbotetal. 2023) resultinan instability ratean orderof magnitude larger,∼1%. Similar discrepancies were also observed byR. E. Zeebe (2015) who studied the position errorof Mer cury when using different Hamiltonian splittings. R. E. Zeebe (2015) correctly concluded that DHC coordinates lack accuracy and Jacobi coordinates are better suitedto the problem. We beginin Sec. 2.1 by running numerical experi ments, which show that this discrepancyis indeed dueto the different coordinate systems usedin the Hamiltonian splitting. We show that simulations using DHC requirea significantly smaller timestepin ordertobe statisti cally converged. In Sec. 2.2, we demonstrate that the stoodin termsof errors notin the position but ratherin the precession rate, as the Solar System’s evolutionis primarily governedby secular dynamics. With this understanding, we show that integrations using DHC become unreliable whena planet’s eccentricity becomes large, or more precisely when the pericenter crossing timescale becomes comparableto the adopted timestep. We showin Sec. 2.3 that the stabilizing effectof the arti ficial numerical precessionis the sameas the stabilizing effect dueto general relativistic (GR) precession. By","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_006,6,"eccentricity becomes large, or more precisely when the pericenter crossing timescale becomes comparableto the adopted timestep. We showin Sec. 2.3 that the stabilizing effectof the arti ficial numerical precessionis the sameas the stabilizing effect dueto general relativistic (GR) precession. By contrast, the splitting using Jacobi coordinates solves the two-body problem for the inner-most planet exactly and reliably reproduces the correct precession rate outto much larger eccentricities. This allows fora signif icantly longer timestep when using Jacobi coordinatesas comparedto DHC. In Sec. 3 we discuss how the pre cession error originates from the integrator’s jump step. We concludein Sec. 4 with some general advice.","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea","1.INTRODUCTION Numerical integrationsof the Solar System, havea",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,134,103,False,False,560,663,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_007,7,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja cobi coordinates perform better than DHC in Solar Sys tem integrations. Thisis because when using Jacobico ordinates for the Hamiltonian splitting, Mercury’smo tionis decomposed intoa Keplerian step ona two-body orbit around the Sun, andan interaction step with the other planets. In the limit where Mercury hasa high eccentricity, the Kepler solver still correctly solves for the Keplerian motion around the Sun-Mercury barycen ter for arbitrarily large timesteps. And because both the orbital timescales and the timescales for planet planet interactions are much longer than the pericenter resolvingTf(see Fig. 1). DHC on the other hand split the Hamiltonian into three parts: Keplerian motion, planet-planet interac tions, andaso-called jump stepor solar term (seeM. J. Duncanetal. 1998; H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2019). The jump step dependson Mercury’s momentum (and thatof the other planets) relativeto the Solar System barycenter. The Keplerian motion solved forin the Ke pler stepis also different from Jacobi case. Mercury does not orbit the",3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_008,8,"like structure seenin the curve for DHC in Fig. 1 is dueto strong timestep resonances (K. P. Rauch & M. Hol man 1999). To suppress these timestep resonances and keep the artificial numerical precession rate below thatof GR we see thatwe needto resolveT fwithat leasta few (∼4) timesteps. The effectwe discuss primarily affects the innermost planet, i.e. Mercuryin the caseof the Solar System. Venus, for example, hasa 2.6 times longer orbital period than Mercury. We are guaranteedto resolve Venus’ peri center timescaleupto eccentricities ofe 2∼0.4 as longaswe resolve Mercury’s orbital period. Furthermore, as soonas the eccentricityof Venus reachese 2∼0.4 it crosses orbits with Mercuryat which point close encoun ters can occur and allof the fixed timestep integratorswe discuss break down.",3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,3.DISCUSSION Our results from Sec.2 show clear evidence that Ja,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,156,120,False,False,280,400,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_009,9,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have been reportedin the literature, ranging from∼0.1% to 1% (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; R. E. Zeebe 2015; D. S. Abbotetal. 2021; D. S. Abbotetal. 2023; N. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond 2025). In this paperwe traced this discrepancy backto the coordinate system usedin the Hamiltonian splittingof the Wisdom-Holman inte grator. We showed that simulations using democratic heliocentric coordinates (DHC) requirea significantly smaller timestepin ordertobe statistically converged comparedto simulations using Jacobi coordinates. The reason for this slow convergenceis the artificial numeri cal precession introducedby DHC whichis stronglyec centricity and timestep dependent. Our results show that DHC should notbe usedin sim ulations which require high accuracy during times when 5More precisely, DHC uses heliocentric positions and barycen tric velocities. SeeM. J. Duncanetal. (1998); H. Rein & D. Tamayo (2019). the eccentricityof planetsis high. The Solar Systemis one example where high accuracyis requiredto measure the correct instability rate because the eccentricityof Mercury becomes large (≳0.4) just before instabilities",4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_010,10,"most scenarios. We have focused our experiments and discussionon the Solar System. However, the general advice for run ning simulationsof any planetary system where one wantsto integrate untilan instability occursis the same:Jacobi coordinates perform significantly better. As soonas orbit crossing occurs and planets can have closeen tenat this pointa simulation canbe stopped and the system canbe considered unstable. Butifitis necessaryto integrate further, thena switch toa hybrid symplec tic integrator ora brute force non-symplectic integra tor canbe madeat this point. Since hybrid integrators workin DHC, itis importantto check that the timestepis small enough, at the very leastdt≲Tf/4, or ideallydt≲Tf/17 as suggested byJ. Wisdom (2015).",4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,4.CONCLUSIONS Different instability ratesof the Solar System have,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,134,103,False,False,280,383,True,4.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_011,11,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof the Solar System with different integrators, Hamilto nian splittings, and timesteps and measure the insta bility ratein each set. Each simulationis slightly per turbedby changing the positionof Earthon the order ofa few metersto obtaina setof equally valid realizations. The results are shownin Table 2, together with previous resultsin the literature. We see that integrations using Jacobi coordinates all agree with the instability rateof∼1% determined byJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009). However, our simula tions with DHC and timestepsof∼6 days suppress all instabilities. The fact thatwe reproduce this result with different integrators and agree with the integrations ofN. A. Kaib & S. N. Raymond (2025) is reassuringasit shows this effectis not due toa specific implementation. Importantly, we are ableto reproduce the correctin stability rate with DHC simulations that usean orderof magnitude smaller timestepof 0.6 days (bottom row). timestep. We will show more evidence for this inter pretationin the next section. Table 1.Comparisonof","2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof","2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_012,12,"wheren 1is the mean motionof Mercury (J. Wisdom 2015). Note that the pericenter timescale canbe sig nificantly shorter than the orbital period for eccentric orbits. One can see that the WH integrator with Jacobico ordinates does not result ina significant precessioner ror for timesteps≲10 days. On the other hand, the WH integrator with DHC introducesan artificialnu merical precession whichis strongly dependenton both the timestep and the eccentricityof Mercury. For large eccentricities, the artificial numerical precession ratebe comes larger than the GR precession rate. Previousstudies have shown that the instability fractionis sen sitiveto GR and any additional precession (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). We therefore expecta significant effecton the stability rate from such numerical effects. We note that the artificial numerical precession has the same directionas the physical GR precession, and that the breakdownof the WH integrator with DHC occurs when the timestep becomes comparableto the pericenter-crossing timescale,dt≳Tf/4. We thus con convergenceof simulations with DHC. 2.3.A physical cause for the different instability rates Our results above suggest that simulations using DHC only break down",2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_013,13,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rateof instabilitiesin long-term integrationsof the Solar System Hanno Rein ,1, 2, 3Kavi Dey ,4and Daniel Tamayo 1Dept. of Physical and Environmental Sciences, Universityof Torontoat Scarborough, Toronto, Ontario, M1C 1A4, Canada 2Dept. of Physics, Universityof Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 3H4, Canada 3Dept. of Astronomy and Astrophysics, Universityof Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, M5S 3H4, Canada 4Dept. of Physics, Harvey Mudd College, 301 Platt Blvd., Claremont 91711, USA",Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rateof instabilitiesin long-term integrationsof the Solar System,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rateof instabilitiesin long-term integrationsof the Solar System,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,110,84,False,False,0,84,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_014,14,"Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System 2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND package (H. Rein & S. F. Liu 2012). REBOUND in cludes several integrators, in particular the WH integra tor WHFast (H. Rein & D. Tamayo 2015) which allowsusto choose the coordinate system for the Hamiltonian splitting. We also run simulations with the WHFast512 integrator (P. Javaherietal. 2023), which only supports DHC. We include comparisons with WHFast512 becauseit does not reuse any code from WHFast, and can there forebe seenasan independent implementation ofa WHintegrator with DHC. This helps rule out any errorsin the implementation asa cause for the observed discrep ancies. For all simulationswe use the present-day positions and velocitiesof all eight Solar System planetsas initial conditions. We combine each planet’s mass with thatof any satellites intoa single point particle locatedat the planet-satellite barycenter. We ignore stellar mass loss, the solar quadrupole, tides, and all minor planets and asteroids. Wedo however include general relativis tic correctionsin the formofan additional potential (A. Nobili & I. W. Roxburgh 1986; D.",2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND,2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_015,15,"point particle locatedat the planet-satellite barycenter. We ignore stellar mass loss, the solar quadrupole, tides, and all minor planets and asteroids. Wedo however include general relativis tic correctionsin the formofan additional potential (A. Nobili & I. W. Roxburgh 1986; D. Tamayoetal. 2020) as the additional precession rateis very importantto correctly capture the long-term stabilityof the Solar System (J. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009; G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). Numerical experiments byD. S. Abbotetal. (2023) suggest that this simplified physical setup incorporates all the relevant physicsto capture thein stability rateof Mercury. We determine ifa systemis unstableby monitoring the eccentricityof planets. Specifically, we definea sys temas unstableifan eccentricity (most likely thatof Mercury) reaches 0.7 at any time during the integra tion. We ran additional tests with different instability criteria, but theydo not change the results significantly (see also Table 1 of the Supplementary Information ofJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau 2009).",2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND,2.TESTS All simulations were performed with the REBOUND,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,194,149,False,False,140,289,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_016,16,"toa specific implementation. Importantly, we are ableto reproduce the correctin stability rate with DHC simulations that usean orderof magnitude smaller timestepof 0.6 days (bottom row). timestep. We will show more evidence for this inter pretationin the next section. Table 1.Comparisonof instability rates for 5 Gyr simulationsof the Solar System using Jacobi (top) vs. democratic heliocentric (bottom) coordinates, usinga rangeof integrators. Integrations are sortedin descending orderby timestep. Additional columns list the numberof runsin each simulation suite, as wellas whether the timestep was decreased once Mercury’s eccentricity rose abovea particular threshold (that variesby paper). The final column lists the percentageof casesin which Mercury goes unstable, with errors denoting 1σcounting statistics. Integrationsin Jacobi coordinates agree statisticallyon the instability rate overa broad rangeof timesteps, while the DHC integrations only convergeon the correct instability rate for timesteps ≲0.6 days. Coordinates Integrator Timestep Runs VarieddtReference Instability rate Jacobi SABA 9.3 days 2501 YesJ. Laskar & M. Gastineau (2009) 0.9%±0.1% Jacobi HNBody 4 days 1600 YesR. E. Zeebe (2015) 0.6%±0.2% Jacobi WHFast 3.16 days 2750 YesD. S. Abbotetal. (2023) 1.0%±0.2%","2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof","2.1.Instability ratein 5 Gyr integrations Asa first test, we run setsof 5 Gyr integrationsof",,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,225,173,False,False,140,313,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_017,17,"the WH integrator with DHC occurs when the timestep becomes comparableto the pericenter-crossing timescale,dt≳Tf/4. We thus con convergenceof simulations with DHC. 2.3.A physical cause for the different instability rates Our results above suggest that simulations using DHC only break down when eccentricities reach large values. To explore this further, we take oneof the unstable Solar System integrations and restartit 5 Myrs priorto the eccentricityof Mercury reaching large (0.7) values. We then integrateit forwardin time for 20 Myrs. We once again run simulations with different coordinate systems and timesteps. This timewe also vary the general rela sion), to 1 (normal GR precession), to 2 (double the GR precession). We run 100 simulations for each case and slightly perturb again the positionof Earthto obtaina setof equally valid realizations. This setup letsus quickly measure the instability rate foran ensembleof simulations. We choseto restart sim ulations 5 Myrs before the instability, roughly one Lya punov timescale (J. Laskar 1989), to give individual sim ulations timeto diverge slightly through phase space. If 1 2 46 10 20 40 dt [days]105 1011031051 ['' / Julian century]",2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_018,18,"Democratic Heliocentric Coordinates Figure 2.Left panel shows Mercury’s eccentricity asa functionof timein 640 integrations using Jacobi coordinates, exhibiting near vertical take-offs when the frequency dominantly associated with Mercury’s eccentricity precessiong 1becomes resonant with thatof Jupiterg 5. Right panel shows the same for 640 integrations using democratic heliocentric coordinates, where the vertical takeoffs are suppressedby artificial precession (see text). Both setsof integrations usea 6 day timestep. Table 2.Instability ratein simulations restarted just prior toa phaseof high eccentricityin Mercury’s orbit. Coordinates Timestep GR Instability rate Jacobi 6 days 1.0 100% Jacobi 6 days 2.0 0% Jacobi 6 days 0.0 100% DHC 6 days 1.0 0% DHC 0.6 days 1.0 100% we wereto restart them much earlier, then the small per turbationswe addto the initial conditions would move most trajectories away from the high eccentricity phasewe aimto explore. Our results are shownin Table 2. First, note that justasin Sect. 2.1, when usinga 6 day timestep, DHC inte-grations again suppress all instabilities. The instability rateof Jacobi coordinatesis only recovered when the timestep for DHC is reducedto 0.6 days. Second, the runs with Jacobi coordinates",2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,560,740,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_019,19,"2. First, note that justasin Sect. 2.1, when usinga 6 day timestep, DHC inte-grations again suppress all instabilities. The instability rateof Jacobi coordinatesis only recovered when the timestep for DHC is reducedto 0.6 days. Second, the runs with Jacobi coordinates and dou reaffirms thata changein the precession rateof order ∼40′′/Julian centuryis sufficientto alter the instabil ity rate (G. Brown & H. Rein 2023). When turning off GR precession completelyin the simulations with Ja cobi coordinates, we still seean instability rateof 100%. This shows that the signof this change matters. More over, because the artificial numerical precession presentin DHC simulationsisin the same directionas the GR precession, we can conclude that the artificial precessionis making the simulations more stablein the same way that the GR precession does. We can connect this directlyto the dynamics behind these instabilities. Chaos causes the precession frequen ciesof the secular modesin the Solar Systemto diffuseon Gyr-timescales, particularly the frequencyg 1associ ated with Mercury (e.g., N. H. Hoangetal. 2021). G. Bou´ eetal. (2012) showed that Mercuryis lost when g1diffuses from its present day valueof∼560′′/Julian century downto∼425′′/Julian century, where",2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,234,180,False,False,700,880,False,2.0
astrobench_25d49d337f,astrobench_25d49d337f_chunk_020,20,"ciesof the secular modesin the Solar Systemto diffuseon Gyr-timescales, particularly the frequencyg 1associ ated with Mercury (e.g., N. H. Hoangetal. 2021). G. Bou´ eetal. (2012) showed that Mercuryis lost when g1diffuses from its present day valueof∼560′′/Julian century downto∼425′′/Julian century, where the sepa ratrixof the secularg 1−g5resonance appears, and Mer cury’s eccentricity approaches unity effectively instanta neously. This rapid evolutionon the secular timescale, whichis much shorter than the Gyr timescale for chaotic diffusion, gives riseto the near-vertical unstable trajec toriesin the left panelof Fig. 2 wherewe plot the eccen tricityof Mercury asa functionof timein simulations using Jacobi coordinates (left) and DHC (right). Asg 1approaches resonancein the DHC integra tions and the eccentricityof Mercury startsto rise, the eccentricity-dependent artificial precession (Fig. 1) raisesg 1and detunesit away from resonance, remov ing the separatrix and suppressing further eccentricity growth. In the right panelof Fig. 2 we see severalin stances where the eccentricityof Mercury startsto rise, onlyto fall back downas the artificial precession moves the system away from resonance. This self-limitingbe haviour thus shuts down the primary pathwayto Mer cury’s instability.",2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,2.2.Artificial numerical precession We next runa setof simulations for 105yrs with both,,,,,Democratic heliocentric coordinates underestimate the rate of instabilities in long-term integrations of the Solar System,"Hanno Rein, Kavi Dey, Daniel Tamayo",astro-ph.EP,2026-01-12T21:51:55Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08019v1,2601.08019,action_angle_methods,229,176,False,False,840,1016,False,2.0
