astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_006,6,"false. Similarly, the PSLI isnotequivalentto StatementB: The observer instantIis equally likelyto havea future that matches thatof anyof theob server instantsI iin the ensemble that are LIKEI. StatementB is equivalentto StatementA, since the statement thatI ihasa future that matches that ofIis equivalentto the statement thatIhasa future that matches that ofIi. Thus, StatementB is also false. Our statementof the PSLI is not phrasedin termsof the equalityof probabilities, but can beversein proportionto the numberof observer-instants LIKEIthatit contains.9 The PSLI canbe implementedin operational termsby whatwe call theMethodof Many Repetitions. In this contextwe imagine some proposition pertainingtoan observer-instant, suchas the proposition that the card that was just drawnby the observerO(I) wasa jackof spades, or the proposition thatO(I) is abouttobe hitby lightning. The method stipulates that the first person probability for any such propositionis equalto the fractionof observer-instantsin the same subjective statein the ensemble for which the propositionis true. The plan for this paperisas follows. In Sec. II, tobe explicit about the general probabilistic settingin whichwe willbe working, we describe the standard Bayesian framework for theory assessment. In Sec. III, we develop and motivate thePrincipleof Self-Locating",I.INTRODUCTIONA striking possibility suggestedby the theories that,I.INTRODUCTIONA striking possibility suggestedby the theories that,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,2660,2840,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_007,7,"there area larger numberof observers and leadsto the patent absurdity knownas the “pre sumptuous philosopher” problem (see Bostrom [11, p. 124])—the problem that any hypothesis can claima high prior probability simplyby presumptuously appendingan assumption thatit gives rise toa colossal numberof observers. We endorse weightingby the numberof observersin the calculationof the first-person probabilities predicted bya given theory, but this does not leadto this absurdity. large universe, these novelties canbe treated pragmati callyby the same Bayesian methods that are commonly usedin empirical physics. We assume thePrincipleof Self-Locating Indifference, butwe regard this assumption asa natural extensionof the typicality assumptions that have always been usedin statistical data analysis. Thisisan optimistic stance (!), considering how littlewe know about the possibility ofa multiverse.",I.INTRODUCTIONA striking possibility suggestedby the theories that,I.INTRODUCTIONA striking possibility suggestedby the theories that,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,153,117,False,False,3080,3197,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_008,8,"who has accessto the entire universe, and first-person probabilities, that wouldbe measuredby any particular real observer within the universe, tak ing into account all selection effects associated with the observer. We have madea specific proposal for how any particular observer should calculate the first-person prob abilities for what she will experience.We proposed that such first-person probabilities shouldbe usedto update probabilities assignedto competing theories, using the completely standard Bayesian procedure. We focusedon the principles that define this method, ignoring any prac tical issues associated with implementingit. We believe thatitis importantto settle these issuesof principle first, and later one can seek practical approximations. We describeda generic theoryTasa system that pro videsa setof possible universes{U α}, anda probability Pcos(Uα) that any oneof them describes the real uni verseU real. EachU αisa complete descriptionof the entire spacetime, and everythinginit. We then imag ined constructinga theory-generated ensembleof possi ble universes, with each possible universe contributingto the ensemblein proportion toP cos(Uα). To make predictions for any particular observerO, one must take into account all selection effects. We argued thatin principle each observeris subjectto her own selec",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_009,9,"theory-generated ensembleof possi ble universes, with each possible universe contributingto the ensemblein proportion toP cos(Uα). To make predictions for any particular observerO, one must take into account all selection effects. We argued thatin principle each observeris subjectto her own selec tion effects, and that these selection effects canin general dependon time. We concluded that predictions shouldbe made separately for each observer-instantI, refer ring toa particular observerOata particular timet. Predictions canbe madeby associatinga given observer instantIin the real universe witha reference classof observer-instantsI iin the theory-generated ensemble. We believe that the best methodof prediction should take into account all available information, sowe defined thesubjective stateSofan observer-instantas the setof all properties that the observer believes, at timet, about herself and her surroundings. The reference class then consistsof all observer instantsI iin the same subjective state asI. There willin generalbe many differentIi’sin differ ent model universesU αin the ensemble, sowe needa procedureto extract predictions from the set. By these definitions the real observerOcannot, at timet, dis tinguish anyof the observer-instantsI ifrom her ownof themis more likelyto describe her future than any other. These",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_010,10,"ent model universesU αin the ensemble, sowe needa procedureto extract predictions from the set. By these definitions the real observerOcannot, at timet, dis tinguish anyof the observer-instantsI ifrom her ownof themis more likelyto describe her future than any other. These are precisely the conditions under which the principleof indifferenceis usually takento apply. In this contextwe propose aPrincipleof Self-Locating In difference(PSLI), which holds thatan observer should predict first-person probabilities for her futureasif her current observer-instant were chosen randomly and uni formly from the observer-instantsin the ensemble that arein the same subjective state. We pointed out that thisis analogous what physicists assumein standard labora tory settings, whereitis always assumed that the results obtainedin the experiment are typical, within expected statistical fluctuations, of the results that any experimentof the same type would yield. We believe that the PSLI isa very reasonable principle, butwe also argue that using this method optimizes the accuracyof predictions. Ina probabilistic system thereis never any wayto guarantee thata specific prediction will turn outtobe true, butwe argued (in AppendixA) that the useof the PSLI by real observers maximizes the fractionof",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_011,11,"also argue that using this method optimizes the accuracyof predictions. Ina probabilistic system thereis never any wayto guarantee thata specific prediction will turn outtobe true, butwe argued (in AppendixA) that the useof the PSLI by real observers maximizes the fractionof them who will make correct predictionsif the theoryis correct. Whether oneis discussing the out comes for the flippingof idealized fair coinsor for experi ments ina very large universe, one can predict what most observers will find, but one cannot guarantee what any particular observer will find. In either case, we believe thatitis sensibleto assume that what most observers will findis most likely whatI will find. Some authors have argued that there isa “random process” thatmo tivates this assumption for the caseof coin flipping, but not for the self-locating uncertainties involvedin predict ing experimental outcomes ina very large universe. In between these two situations.A possibly controversial consequenceof the PSLI is the “weightingby the numberof observers”—thatis, in determining first-person probabilities, the weight associ ated with any possible model universeU αis proportionalto both its third-person probabilityP cos(Uα) andto the numberof observer-instantsit contains that",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,560,740,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_012,12,"these two situations.A possibly controversial consequenceof the PSLI is the “weightingby the numberof observers”—thatis, in determining first-person probabilities, the weight associ ated with any possible model universeU αis proportionalto both its third-person probabilityP cos(Uα) andto the numberof observer-instantsit contains that arein the same subjective stateas the observer-instant for which predictions are sought. In contrast, Hartle and collabo rators (in Refs. [8, 13, 20, 39, 67], for example) advocate weighting each possible model universe onlyby its third person probability, restrictedto universes that haveat least one instanceof our dataD.18In AppendixB, we show how the weightingby observers canbe derived froma setof simpler, reasonable assumptions. large universesis relevantis the questionof “Boltz mann brains”. In Sec. IV-C, we argued that theoriesin which Boltzmann brains dominate over ordinaryob servers shouldbe rejected. They shouldbe rejected not becausewe seea rational reason why they cannotbe valid, but instead because, if they are valid, thenwe would most likelybe Boltzmann brains and would not that such theories canbe useful for scientific investiga tions. in this paper, but leave asa topic for future investigation, is the time evolutionof subjective states and its",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,True,False,700,880,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_013,13,"if they are valid, thenwe would most likelybe Boltzmann brains and would not that such theories canbe useful for scientific investiga tions. in this paper, but leave asa topic for future investigation, is the time evolutionof subjective states and its possible relevanceto the calculationof first-person probabilities. One possibilityis that subjective states are well-modeledas being discrete, soan observer evolves fora shortpe riodof timein one subjective state, and then transitions instantaneously toa subsequent state. In that case, it mightbe appropriateto calculate first-person probabil itiesby weighting these intervalsin proportionto their duration, rather than assuming that all observer-instants count equally. Alternatively, it mightbe betterto treat the time evolutionof subjective states asa continuous evolution, in which case one might consider whether the weight giventoan observer-instant might dependon how quickly the observer passes through the specified subjec tive state. The methods developedin this paper are relevantto several interesting questions whichwe have not discussed here. Oneis the questionof whether evidence that the universeis fine-tunedto allow for the possibilityof life shouldbe interpretedas evidencein favor ofa multiverse. Anotheris theso-called “Doomsday Argument”—theas 18The definitionof “data” usedby Hartle and collaborators seems",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,840,1020,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_014,14,"several interesting questions whichwe have not discussed here. Oneis the questionof whether evidence that the universeis fine-tunedto allow for the possibilityof life shouldbe interpretedas evidencein favor ofa multiverse. Anotheris theso-called “Doomsday Argument”—theas 18The definitionof “data” usedby Hartle and collaborators seems very similarto our definitionof “subjective state”. In Ref. [8], the authors describe “the dataD 0that the HSI [human scientific IGUS, where IGUS = information gathering and utilizing system] has: every scrapof information that the HSI possesses about the physical universe, including every recordof every experiment, every astronomical observationof distant galaxies, every avail able descriptionof every leaf, etc., and necessarily every pieceof information about the HSI itself, its members, and its history.” sertion that human civilizationis not likelyto survive much longer than its current age, sincea much longer periodof survival would placeus atypically earlyinhu man history. We leave these questionsas topics for future research. Note that this paperis directly relevantto very large However, many cosmological theories under considera tion today describe infinite “multiverses,” where the def initionof probabilities requiresa solutionto whatis knownas the measure problem. Of course there remains much worktobe",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,True,True,980,1160,True,5.0
astrobench_3644e67b5c,astrobench_3644e67b5c_chunk_015,15,"Probabilistic inference in very large universes V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa tings have ledto confusion and controversyin the litera ture. We are interestedin theories that describe universesin which the effective, low-energy approximationsto the lawsof physics can vary from one regionto another, so the measurementsof any observer will strongly dependon where she lives. Furthermore, the local low-energy lawsof physicsin any region can havea strong influenceon whether observers existin the region. If observersdo exist, their number and their characteristics will alsobe strongly influencedby the local low-energy laws. In given observer might experience, or for how she should assess the credibilityof competing theories. In this paperwe have argued that these questions are notas novelas some authors have claimed, and that the best methodof analysisis closely analogousto standard methodsof physics. We distinguish between third-person probabilities, that wouldbe measured bya hypothetical external observer who has accessto the entire universe, and first-person probabilities, that wouldbe measuredby any particular real observer within the universe, tak ing into account all selection effects associated with the observer. We have madea specific proposal for how any particular observer",V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,V.CONCLUSION Probabilistic reasoningin very large universes isa,,,,,Probabilistic inference in very large universes,"Feraz Azhar, Alan H. Guth, Mohammad Hossein Namjoo",astro-ph.CO,2026-02-02T19:00:05Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02667v1,2602.02667,distribution_functions,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,4.0
