astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_011,11,"the Osipkov–Merritt distri bution function for any spherical model (for details, see Baesetal. 2021; Baes 2023), we can numerically calculate𝜉 crit(𝑟T,𝜆)for each (𝑟T,𝜆)combination. The resultis shownin Fig. 5 for three different values of𝑟 T. At fixed𝑟 T,𝜉critisa decreasing function of𝜆, which implies that models with more tangential orbital structures allow sharper truncations. This isa logical consequenceof the fact thatitis easierto populate spherical models with nearly circular than with highly eccentric orbits. In the limit𝜆𝑟2 T=−1or𝜆=𝜆 min, we find 𝜉crit=1for all𝑟 T, which means that all truncations are allowed, including the infinitely sharp truncation. An interesting observationis that smoothly truncated models allowa rangeof orbital struc tures,includingradiallyanisotropicOsipkov–Merrittmodels.Thisis againa stark difference from sharply truncated models, where only the extreme tangential Osipkov–Merritt model with𝜆=−1/𝑟2 Tis consistent (Paper II). Asaconcreteexample,weconsiderthetruncatedHernquistmodel with𝑟 T=4and𝜉=0.6.Forthismodel,allOsipkov–Merrittmodels with−0.0625⩽𝜆⩽0.1828are consistent. On the bottom rowof Fig. 6 we show the distribution function for the most tangential, the isotropic, and the most radial modelsin this range. The distribution functions are shownin turning-point space, which allows foran easier comparison than when shown asa function of𝑄, since the quantity𝑄depends explicitlyon the value of𝜆. The top","4.2 Extensionto anisotropic orbital structures So farwe have focusedon isotropic dynamical models, the simplest","4.2 Extensionto anisotropic orbital structures So farwe have focusedon isotropic dynamical models, the simplest",,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,234,180,False,True,420,600