astrobench_3474c47fe1,astrobench_3474c47fe1_chunk_024,24,"that theex istenceofapositiveandwell-behaveddistributionfunctiondoesnot, by itself, guarantee long-term stability. Adefinitiveassessmentofthestabilityofsoftlytruncatedmodels, both isotropic and anisotropic, requiresa dedicated investigation using linear stability analysis and/or controlled𝑁-body simulations withinitialconditionsdrawndirectlyfromthedistributionfunctions presented here. Sucha study would allow oneto quantify growth rates,identifythedominantmodes,andexplorehowstabilitydependson truncation radius, truncation sharpness, and orbital anisotropy. This lies beyond the scopeof the present paper, whose primary aimisto establish phase-space consistency andto providea general framework for constructing finite-extent equilibrium models.",4.3 Stability considerations An important caveat concerns the dynamical stabilityof the softly,4.3 Stability considerations An important caveat concerns the dynamical stabilityof the softly,,,,,Self-consistent dynamical models with a finite extent -- V. Smooth radial truncations and phase-space consistency,Maarten Baes,astro-ph.GA,2026-01-13T09:17:21Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08361v1,2601.08361,distribution_functions,84,64,False,False,420,484,False,1.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_000,0,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten tialis centralto connecting stellar kinematicsto the Galaxy’s mass distribution and secular evolution ( J. Bland-Hawthorn & O. Gerhard 2016 ). Evenin the solar neighborhood, degeneracies persist between the halo shape and the disk and bulge/bar contributions. While the dark matter halois often approximated bya spherical NFW profile ( J. F. Navarroetal. 1997 ), its precise geometry remainsa subjectof ongoingde bate. Many recent analyses favora flattened, oblate configuration ( q < 1) to better match kinematic con straints ( Y. Huangetal. 2024 ), although alternative models suggestinga prolate shape have also been pro posed ( L. Zhuetal. 2026 ). In the disk, additionalun certainty arises from non-axisymmetric structure: the Galactic bar pattern speed remains debated, with “fast” (∼55 kms−1kpc−1) and “slow” ( ∼40 kms−1kpc−1) so lutions supportedby different dynamical tracers ( W. Dehnen 1999;J. L. Sandersetal. 2019;J. P. Clarke Corresponding author: LongW ang wanglong8@sysu.edu.cnetal. 2019a;S. Lucchinietal. 2024;J. A. S. Hunt & E. V asiliev 2025 ). The morphology and dynamical natureof",1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_001,1,"( ∼40 kms−1kpc−1) so lutions supportedby different dynamical tracers ( W. Dehnen 1999;J. L. Sandersetal. 2019;J. P. Clarke Corresponding author: LongW ang wanglong8@sysu.edu.cnetal. 2019a;S. Lucchinietal. 2024;J. A. S. Hunt & E. V asiliev 2025 ). The morphology and dynamical natureof the spiral arms are likewise uncertain ( R. J. J. Grandetal. 2012;G. H. Hunteretal. 2024;Y. R. Khaliletal. 2025 ). Stellar tidal streams—formedby the gradual strippingof star clusters—provide phase-coherent tracersof the Galactic potential ( S. E. Koposovetal. 2010;S. L. J. Gibbonsetal. 2014 ). Globular-cluster streams are typically narrow and prominent (e.g., M. Odenkirchenetal. 2001 ), but they often probe the potentialat larger Galactocentric distances. In contrast, open cluster streams residein the disk and are therefore par ticularly sensitiveto perturbations from the bar, spi ral arms, and encounters with giant molecular clouds (M. Gielesetal. 2006;K. Hattorietal. 2016;N. C. Amoriscoetal. 2016 ). Their low surface density and strong field-star contamination make them challengingto identify, but the astrometric precisionof Gaia now enables systematic searches for these nearby, diffuse structures. The Hyadesisan exceptional laboratorybe cause its proximity permitsa fine-grained comparison between observed tidal tails and",1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_002,2,"low surface density and strong field-star contamination make them challengingto identify, but the astrometric precisionof Gaia now enables systematic searches for these nearby, diffuse structures. The Hyadesisan exceptional laboratorybe cause its proximity permitsa fine-grained comparison between observed tidal tails and dynamical models ( S. Observational efforts initially focusedon the Hyades core, using proper-motion catalogs and Hipparcosas trometryto characterize membership within the tidal radius ( M. A. C. Perrymanetal. 1998 ). S. Röseretal. (2011 ) extended the search using the PPMXL catalog, providing early evidence that the cluster extends beyond its classical tidal boundary. The extended tails became accessible with Gaia (Gaia Collaborationetal. 2016, 2018;S. Meingastetal. 2019 ). In particular, S. Röseretal. (2019 ) mapped tidal-tail membersin Gaia DR2 using the convergent point (CP) method, establishingan empirical reference for the stream orientation near the cluster. Numerical simulations are essential for connecting the present-day stream morphologyto the underlying Galac tic dynamics. EarlyN-body models primarily focusedon reproducing the cluster’s mass-loss rate and the evo lutionof its core properties withina static, axisymmet ric Galactic potential ( Y. O. Chumaketal. 2005;A. Ernstetal. 2011 ). The",1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_003,3,"the present-day stream morphologyto the underlying Galac tic dynamics. EarlyN-body models primarily focusedon reproducing the cluster’s mass-loss rate and the evo lutionof its core properties withina static, axisymmet ric Galactic potential ( Y. O. Chumaketal. 2005;A. Ernstetal. 2011 ). The Gaia detectionof asymmetric tidal tails indicates that local dynamics are more com plex than canbe capturedbyan axisymmetric model ( T. Jerabkovaetal. 2021 ). Recent work has therefore begunto incorporate non-axisymmetric structure; for example, G. F. Thomasetal. (2023 ) explored the influence ofa rotating bar anda flattened halo. However, the joint impactof the bar and spiral arms—and, in particular, how their pattern speeds map into observable stream diagnostics—remains insuﬀiciently explored. In this paperwe performa systematic surveyof bar and spiral pattern speeds using high-resolution PETARN-body simulations ina multi-component AGAMA MilkyW ay potential. We confront the models with Gaia EDR3 using CP/CCP kinematic selection andan adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching procedure. We emphasizea single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution ina stream-aligned coordinate frame, includinga prominent density peakat Yrot≈0.1 kpc. We find that",1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_004,4,"selection andan adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching procedure. We emphasizea single, consistently applied diagnostic across the manuscript: the stream orientation near the cluster and the longitudinal density distribution ina stream-aligned coordinate frame, includinga prominent density peakat Yrot≈0.1 kpc. We find that models with Ωb≃40–45 kms−1kpc−1and Ωs= 22.5 kms−1kpc−1 best reproduce the observed density structure, support inga relatively slow Galactic bar. This paperis organizedas follows. Section 2.1 de scribes the Galactic potential model and theN-body simulation setup. Section 2.3 presents the simulated stream morphologies and kinematics across the explored (Ωb,Ωs)parameter space. In Section 3we compare the simulationsto observations, using the Röser CP-selected sample and Gaia EDR3 to constrain the pattern speeds via stream orientation and density structure. We discuss limitations and future observational testsin Section 4.2. N-BODY SIMULA TIONS",1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,1.INTRODUCTION Constraining the MilkyW ay gravitational poten,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,166,127,False,True,560,687,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_005,5,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 3.2.Convergent Point MethodWe then proceededto investigate the propertiesof the spatially matched modelsin velocity space using the CP method. This approach considers that star clus ter members move asa single entity witha common three-dimensional space velocity vector. Its projection onto the celestial sphere yieldsa convergent point. By adopting the bulk space velocity vectorof the cluster, the CP method predicts the expected transverse motion ofa member starat any given positionon the sky. This predicted motion canbe decomposed into two orthog onal components: one directed toward the convergent point ( V∥,pred ) and one perpendiculartoit ( V⊥,pred ). F ora star perfectly sharing the cluster’s common spaceve locity, the perpendicular componentV⊥,predisby defini tion zero, whileV∥,pred represents the full magnitudeof the projected transverse velocity. By calculating these two velocity components, itis possibleto identify clus ter members. The CP methodis particularly effective for nearby clusters whose members span large areason the sky. F or all spatially matched models, we employed the CP methodto calculate the difference between the trans verse motionof each",3.2.Convergent Point MethodWe then proceededto investigate the propertiesof the,3.2.Convergent Point MethodWe then proceededto investigate the propertiesof the,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_006,6,"possibleto identify clus ter members. The CP methodis particularly effective for nearby clusters whose members span large areason the sky. F or all spatially matched models, we employed the CP methodto calculate the difference between the trans verse motionof each star within the simulated stellar stream and the expected transverse motion, presenting the resultsin CP diagramas shownin Fig. 8. The CP diagrams exhibit notable variationsin length and dis persion across different models. F or comparative pur poses, we have overplotted data from the Röser datasetas gray pointsin the same figures. We note thatal though some field contaminants may stillbe presentin the observational sample, we did not further cleanitus ing Gaia line-of-sight velocities. Thisis because the lim ited availability and relatively high uncertainties ofR VS data for fainter stars would significantly compromise the completenessof the sample. It canbe observed that all models demonstrate similarityto the Röser datasetin the central regions, providing further confirmation that while the extended tidal tails show significant variations among different models, the spatial and kinematic prop ertiesin the core regionsof the stellar streams remain comparable.",3.2.Convergent Point MethodWe then proceededto investigate the propertiesof the,3.2.Convergent Point MethodWe then proceededto investigate the propertiesof the,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,232,178,False,False,140,318,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_007,7,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 5.CONCLUSIONSWe modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream with directN-body simulations ( PETAR ) ina multi component MilkyW ay potential implemented with AGAMA and includinga rotating bar and spiral arms. By systematically varying the bar and spiral pattern speeds, we quantified how non-axisymmetric forcingre shapes the stream orientation, length, and internal den sity structure over the ∼648 Myr evolutionof the clus ter. We then confronted the models with observationsby combining CP/CCP-based kinematic selectionon Gaia EDR3 withan adaptive three-dimensional spatial matching technique. The key observational constraintis the stream-aligned longitudinal density profileof the Gaia candidates. This profile showsa prominent peakat Yrot≈0.1 kpc, whichis consistent with the overdensity reported byT. Jer abkovaetal. (2021 ). We find that this featureis best reproducedby models with Ωs= 22.5 kms−1kpc−1and Ωb≃40–45 kms−1kpc−1, supportinga relatively slow Galactic bar. These findings also indicate that thein clusionof spiral armsis not negligible and Ωsaround this intermediate value are favored. More broadly, our results demonstrate that nearby open-cluster streams providea complementary, local avenueto constrain non-axisymmetric Galactic dynamics using not",5.CONCLUSIONSWe modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,5.CONCLUSIONSWe modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_008,8,"slow Galactic bar. These findings also indicate that thein clusionof spiral armsis not negligible and Ωsaround this intermediate value are favored. More broadly, our results demonstrate that nearby open-cluster streams providea complementary, local avenueto constrain non-axisymmetric Galactic dynamics using not only the global stream orientation but also its fine-grained den sity structure.ACKNOWLEDGMENTSLW thanks the support from the National Natural ScienceF oundationof China through grant 12573041 and 12233013, the High-levelY outhT alent Project (Provincial Financial Allocation) through the grant 2023HYSPT0706, theF undamental ResearchF unds for the Central Universities, SunY at-sen Univer sity (2025QNPY04). TJ acknowledges the support from the MUNIA wardin Science and Humanities (MUNI/I/1762/2023).",5.CONCLUSIONSWe modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,5.CONCLUSIONSWe modeled the formationof the Hyades tidal stream,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,136,104,False,False,140,244,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_009,9,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS ZZ was responsible for the method development, per forming the simulations, data analysis, and writing the manuscript. LW campup with the initialre search concept, contributedto discussions, edited the manuscript, and supervised the overall project. TJ developed the observational-comparison concept, pro vided observational data, contributedto discussions, and edited the manuscript. ZH contributedto the Galactic-potential method development and discussions. Software: NumPy (C. R. Harrisetal. 2020 ), Matplotlib (J. D. Hunter 2007 ), Astropy ( As tropy Collaborationetal. 2013, 2018, 2022 ), SDAR (L. W ang 2020, https://github.com/lwang astro/SDAR), PETAR (L. W angetal. 2020, https://github.com/lwang-astro/PeT ar), FDPS (M. Iwasawaetal. 2016, 2020; D. Namekataetal. 2018, https://github.com/FDPS/FDPS), SSE/BSE (J. R. Hurleyetal. 2000, 2002; S. Banerjeeetal. 2020 ), McLuster (A. H. W. Küpperetal. 2011; L. W angetal. 2019, modified version: https://github.com/lwang-astro/mcluster), AGAMA (E. V asiliev 2019, https://github.com/GalacticDynamics Oxford/Agama), Astroquery (A. Ginsburgetal. 2019 )","AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS ZZ was responsible for the method development, per","AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS ZZ was responsible for the method development, per",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,198,152,False,False,0,152,True,5.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_010,10,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream Zi-yi Zhou,1LongW ang,1, 2Tereza Jerabkova,3and Zhenghao He1 1Schoolof Physics and Astronomy, SunY at-sen University, Daxue Road, 519082 Zhuhai, China 2CSST Science Center for the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macau Greater Bay Area, 519082 Zhuhai, China 3Departmentof Theoretical Physics and Astrophysics, F acultyof Science, Masaryk University, Kotlářská 2, Brno 611 37, Czech Republic ABSTRACTWe presenta suiteof directN-body simulationsof the Hyades open cluster and its tidal stream ina MilkyW ay potential that includesa rotating bar and spiral arms. Using the high-resolution code PETAR andan AGAMA-based multi-component Galactic model, we vary the bar and spiral pattern speeds ( Ωb,Ωs) ona discrete grid and quantify the resulting changesin stream orientation, length, and internal density structure. We compare the simulationsto Gaia EDR3 using the convergent point (CP) and compact convergent point (CCP) methods, followedbyan adaptive three-dimensional nearest neighbor matchingin Cartesian space (x, y, z ). The Gaia candidate members exhibita pronounced longitudinal density peakat Yrot≈0.1 kpc ina stream-aligned coordinate system. Models with",Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_79edfd6850,astrobench_79edfd6850_chunk_011,11,"Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream 2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN body code PETAR (L. W angetal. 2020 ) to simulate the dynamical evolutionof the Hyades ( L. W ang & T. Jer abkova 2021 ). PETAR has the abilityto eﬀiciently handle both the internal collisional dynamicsof the star cluster and the external Galactic tidal field. Specifically, PETAR employsa hybrid algorithm that effectively accounts for gravitational interactions across all spatial scalesby combining dedicated methods for short-, middle-, and long-range forces using sdar and fdps (L. W ang 2020; S. J. Aarseth 2003;J. Barnes & P. Hut 1986;M. Iwa sawaetal. 2016,2020;D. Namekataetal. 2018 ). This multi-scale treatment ensures the high numerical preci sion and computational eﬀiciency requiredto simulateT able 2. Present-day parametersof the Hyades (in the ICRS frame) ParameterV alue RA [deg] 67.985 Dec [deg] 17.012 Distance [pc] 47.501 pm(RA, cos(Dec)) [mas/yr] 101.005 pm(Dec) [mas/yr] −28.490 Radial velocity [km/s] 39.96 Age [Myr] 648 the long-term evolution and mass lossof stellar systems over several hundred megayears. The present-day parameters adopted for","2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN","2.2.Simulation setup In this work, we employed the high-performanceN",,,,,Constraining the Galactic bar and spiral pattern speeds with the Hyades tidal stream,"Zi-yi Zhou, Long Wang, Tereza Jerabkova, Zhenghao He",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-31T07:33:52Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29360v1,2603.29360,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
