astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_000,0,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal 1980; G. Bertone & D. Hooper 2018) isa non-luminous componentof matter whose total abundanceis inferredtobe approximately five times thatof ordinary baryonic matter (D. N. Spergeletal. 2003; C. L. Bennettetal. 2003). It has remaineda central open problemin both particle physics and astrophysics for several decades. Observations indicate that every galaxyis embeddedinan extended DM halo that dominates its mass bud get (J. P. Ostriker & P. J. E. Peebles 1973; J. Einasto 2004; G. Bertone & D. Hooper 2018). Thus, onema jor strategy for probing DM in terrestrial experimentsis direct detection, which aimsto observe rare scattering events between DM particles and Standard Model tar Email: xiuyuan@mit.edugets (A. Drukier & L. Stodolsky 1984; M. W. Goodman & E. Witten 1985; A. K. Drukieretal. 1986; K. Freeseetal. 2013). Allof these searches rely criticallyon the assumed fluxof DM passing through the Earth, whichis determinedby the local DM density and velocity dis tribution. The most commonly adopted assumption for the DM velocity distributionis the Standard Halo",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_001,1,"1986; K. Freeseetal. 2013). Allof these searches rely criticallyon the assumed fluxof DM passing through the Earth, whichis determinedby the local DM density and velocity dis tribution. The most commonly adopted assumption for the DM velocity distributionis the Standard Halo Model (SHM) (A. K. Drukieretal. 1986; J. D. Lewin & P. F. Smith 1996), which describes the Galactic DM haloasan isothermal, spherically symmetric systemin equilib rium. In this framework, the local DM velocities followsa Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution,5and the spherical Jeans’ equation relates the one-dimensional DM veloc 5The isothermal solution does not formally includea cutoff; in practice, a cutoffat the Galactic escape speedis imposed, typ ity dispersionto the circular velocity via σDM=vc/√ 2, wherea flat rotation curve withvc∼220 km /sisas sumed (F. J. Kerr & D. Lynden-Bell 1986; J. Bland Hawthorn & O. Gerhard 2016; A.-C. Eilersetal. 2019). The isothermal profile underlying the SHM assumes ρ∝r−2, with rthe spherical distance from the centerof galaxy, producing this flat rotation curve – a sim plification that deviates from the Milky Way’s actual mass distribution. While this deviationis smallat the solar position, more generally, the",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_002,2,"the SHM assumes ρ∝r−2, with rthe spherical distance from the centerof galaxy, producing this flat rotation curve – a sim plification that deviates from the Milky Way’s actual mass distribution. While this deviationis smallat the solar position, more generally, the local velocity distri bution dependson the halo’s density slope, any radial variationin the velocity dispersion σ, and the velocity anisotropy. Furthermore, the Milky Way’s halois not expectedtobein equilibrium dueto its hierarchicalas sembly history and ongoing merger events, so the true DM velocity distributionis expectedto depart from the Maxwell-Boltzmann form assumedin the SHM. Several groups have used cosmological simulationsto quantify these departures and their consequences fordi rect detection. Using high-resolution Milky Way–like halos, M. Vogelsbergeretal. (2009) found thatnu clear recoil rates can differ from the SHM predictionbyupto 10%. Y.-Y. Maoetal. (2013, 2014) char acterized the halo-to-halo scatterin the local velocity distribution, showing thatit can appreciably shiftex clusion limits and preferred parameter regions. Analy ses basedon Via Lactea–type halos (J. Diemandetal. 2007, 2008) explicitly compared simulation-derivedre coil spectra against SHM predictions, finding that light and inelastic DM scenarios are particularly sensitiveto structurein",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_003,3,"showing thatit can appreciably shiftex clusion limits and preferred parameter regions. Analy ses basedon Via Lactea–type halos (J. Diemandetal. 2007, 2008) explicitly compared simulation-derivedre coil spectra against SHM predictions, finding that light and inelastic DM scenarios are particularly sensitiveto structurein the high-velocity tail (A. Friedland & I. M. Shoemaker 2013). More recently, hydrodynamical sim ulations incorporating baryons have revealed that disk formation and baryonic contraction reshape the local DM phase-space distribution, further modifying direct detection interpretationsN. Bozorgnia & G. Bertone (2017). Taken together, these studies establish that the assumed velocity distribution leadsto systematic uncer taintiesin direct-detection analyses. Recent developmentsin observational astrometry have shown that the Milky Way (MW) has undergone multi ple mergers throughout its history (seee.g., A. Helmi 2020, fora review). The most prominent mergeris the Gaia–Sausage–Enceladus (GSE) (V. Belokurovetal. 2018; A. Helmietal. 2018); the GSE is assumedto have been accretedto the MW about 10 billion years ago, and was the result ofa 1:3 merger (J. W. Johnsonetal. 2025) that contributed around one-fourthof the total massof the MW andis likely responsible for thickening theG. Monarietal. 2018; H. H. Koppelman",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_004,4,"been accretedto the MW about 10 billion years ago, and was the result ofa 1:3 merger (J. W. Johnsonetal. 2025) that contributed around one-fourthof the total massof the MW andis likely responsible for thickening theG. Monarietal. 2018; H. H. Koppelman & A. Helmi 2021; L. Necib & T. Lin 2022; C. Rocheetal. 2024).MW’s disk (A. Helmietal. 2018; H. H. Koppelmanetal. 2020). Other mergers include the currently stripped Sagittarius Dwarf Spheroidal Galaxy (Sgr dSph) (R. A. Ibataetal. 1994; R. A. Ibataetal. 1995), as wellas the infalling satellites like the Large and Small Magellanic Cloud (LMC and SMC) (N. Kallivayaliletal. 2006; G. Beslaetal. 2007) that also potentially affects the local DM velocity distribution (G. Beslaetal. 2019). It was then proposed inJ. Herzog-Arbeitmanetal. (2018) that the stellar velocity distribution can serve asa proxy for the full DM velocity distribution. Sub sequently, using two Milky Way–like galaxies from the FIRE-2 simulations (A. R. Wetzeletal. 2016; P. F. Hop kinsetal. 2018), L. Necibetal. (2019) demonstrateda correlation between the velocity distributionsof differ ent stellar populations and distinct DM componentsin the solar neighborhood. In particular, DM and",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_005,5,"Milky Way–like galaxies from the FIRE-2 simulations (A. R. Wetzeletal. 2016; P. F. Hop kinsetal. 2018), L. Necibetal. (2019) demonstrateda correlation between the velocity distributionsof differ ent stellar populations and distinct DM componentsin the solar neighborhood. In particular, DM and stars associated with early mergers (at redshiftz≳3) are dynamically relaxed and therefore exhibita strong cor respondence. By contrast, material from more recent mergersis only partially phase-mixed, leaving behind debris flows and cold streams. While the stellar–DM correspondence remains adequate for debris-flow com ponents, it becomes significantly less reliable for cold streams (with infall timesz < 1) dueto their pro nounced spatial inhomogeneity. Consequently, a more sophisticated treatmentis requiredto reconstruct the underlying DM velocity distribution from stellar trac ers. Subsequent work inT. Shpigeletal. (2025) showed that, in the presence ofa single late-time merger, itis nevertheless possibleto recover the DM speed distribu tion accuratelyby incorporatinga simple boost factor into the stellar-based reconstruction. In this work, we investigate seven m12FIRE galax ies, comprisinga totalof 24 major mergers, to establisha concrete and self-consistent reconstructionof thelo cal DM velocity distribution. We beginby decomposing the local",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_006,6,"structuredas the traceable mergers and the relax component, fDM(v) =4Xi=1cifi(v) + (1 −4Xi=1ci)frelaxed (v),(1) where ciis the mass fractionof local DM from thei-th traceable merger, fiis the corresponding velocity dis tribution, and frelaxedis the velocity distributionof the relaxed component. We establisha connection between the stellar and DM velocity distributions originating from the same merger, and characterize how this connection varies with accre tion time. We model the diffuse component asa gener alized Gaussian function and demonstrate, using simu lations, that the total local DM velocity distribution canbe robustly reconstructed. We assess both the sensitiv ityof this reconstructionto the numerical resolutionof the simulations and the main sourcesof error that would ariseinan applicationto the Milky Way. Alternatively, we also demonstrate that the general ized Gaussianby itself canbe directly usedasanap proximationof the full velocity distribution. In termsof speed distribution, it isa simple yet consistentim provement over SHM. However, it might failto predict the azimuthal velocity distributionin the presenceofco-rotation. This paperis organizedas follows. In Sec. 2, we intro duce the FIRE simulation suite: we summarize the key propertiesof the various m12galaxiesin Sec. 2.1, andde",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,980,1160,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_007,7,"consistentim provement over SHM. However, it might failto predict the azimuthal velocity distributionin the presenceofco-rotation. This paperis organizedas follows. In Sec. 2, we intro duce the FIRE simulation suite: we summarize the key propertiesof the various m12galaxiesin Sec. 2.1, andde scribe our methodology for associating DM and stellar particles with individual mergers and tracing them backin timein Sec. 2.2. In Sec. 3, we start witha discus sionof the merger component and examine the prop ertiesof DM and stellar components associated with major mergers. We beginby introducing the statis tical frameworkin Sec. 3.1.1, analyze the dependenceof stellar–DM correlationson merger accretion timesin Sec. 3.1.2, and presenta robust comparisonof the full three-dimensional velocity distributionsin Sec. 3.1.3. We further investigate the useof boost factorsto better align stellar and DM velocity distributionsin Sec. 3.1.4. In Sec. 4, we turnto the diffuse DM component, show ing thatit dominates the local DM budget andis well described bya generalized Gaussian velocity distribu tion. Buildingon this, Sec. 5 outlines our method forre constructing the local DM speed distribution from stel lar kinematic data alone. Additionally, we demonstrate that the",1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,1.INTRODUCTION Dark matter (DM) (F.Zwicky 1933; V.C.Rubinetal,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1120,1300,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_008,8,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct ing the local DM velocity distributionby leveraginga galaxy’s accretion history, guidedby stellar kinematics and insights from high-resolution cosmological simula tions. We decompose the DM population into four dis tinct components—diffuse DM, top mergers traceableby stellar debris, and both young and old untraceable merg ers—and constructa composite model that integrates both parametric descriptions and data-driven elementsto capture the full kinematic complexityof the halo. We show that the diffuse DM componentis well char acterizedby generalized Gaussian profiles, while the con tributions from major mergers canbe effectively mod eledby convolving stellar velocity distributions with Gaussian kernels. Using the EMD asa quantitativedi agnostic, we find that our reconstruction consistently outperforms the Standard Halo Model (SHM), and that the generalized SHM (gSHM) alone also representsa sig nificant improvement over SHM. However, dueto cur rent limitationsin modeling the DM mass contribution from individual mergers—primarily stemming fromin complete understandingof the accretion and tidal dis ruption processes—the full reconstruction does notex hibita systematic advantage over gSHM.","7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct","7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_009,9,"also representsa sig nificant improvement over SHM. However, dueto cur rent limitationsin modeling the DM mass contribution from individual mergers—primarily stemming fromin complete understandingof the accretion and tidal dis ruption processes—the full reconstruction does notex hibita systematic advantage over gSHM. Consequently, we recommend the generalized SHM as the most robust and practical refinementof the SHM for usein future DM direct-detection analyses. For directional signals and annual modulation probes, we recommend the full reconstruction. This approach highlights the promiseof using stellar information asa proxy for the local dark matter struc ture, while also making clear that the dominant uncer tainty liesin estimating the amountof dark matter con tributedby each merger. In practice, this requires infer ring the accreted dark matter fraction from the stellar component through the light-to-mass ratio and abun dance matching, bothof which remain intrinsicallyun certain and are not expectedto improve dramaticallyin the near term. Asa result, our reconstructionis already closeto the best that can presentlybe achieved within this framework. Meaningful further progress will likely requirea better theoretical and numerical understand ingof tidal disruption, mass deposition, and the relation","7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct","7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_010,10,"expectedto improve dramaticallyin the near term. Asa result, our reconstructionis already closeto the best that can presentlybe achieved within this framework. Meaningful further progress will likely requirea better theoretical and numerical understand ingof tidal disruption, mass deposition, and the relation between stellar and dark matter strippingin hierarchical galaxy formation. Furthermore, the best-fit βvaluesin the gSHM are consistently similar across the angular directions, while the radial direction exhibitsa systematically higher β, indicatinga more boxy distribution with suppressed tails. This anisotropy may reflect the effectsof violent relaxation along the radial direction, hinting ata statis tical mechanics origin for the generalized Gaussian form. The consistencyof these features across different simu lations and resolutions points towardan emergentdy namical equilibrium. Further theoretical investigations are warrantedto explore the origin and generalityof this behavior. The reconstruction pipelineas wellas the generalized SHM canbe straightforwardly appliedto observational data and thus modify the implied MW local DM veloc ity distribution. The implicationsof these new velocity distributionson DM direct detection experiments willbe exploredin subsequent works.","7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct","7.CONCLUSIONS In this work, we presenta framework for reconstruct",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,214,164,False,False,280,444,True,5.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_011,11,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS XZ led the analysis, developed the methodology, wrote the analysis code, and drafted the manuscript. AT im plemented and debugged the stellar and dark matter assignment pipeline, carried out validation tests, and wrote the appendices and associated sections. LN con ceived the project, provided the initial codebase, and supervised the analysis throughout. AW and AA pro vided feedbackon the methodology and results within the frameworkof the FIRE collaboration. All authors reviewed and commentedon the manuscript. Software: NumPy (C. R. Harrisetal. 2020), SciPy (P. Virtanenetal. 2020), Matplotlib (J. D. Hunter 2007), Pandas (W. McKinney 2010; T. pandas devel opment team 2020), Astropy ( Astropy Collaborationetal. 2013, 2018, 2022), GizmoAnalysis (A. Wetzel & S. Garrison-Kimmel 2020a), HaloAnalysis (A. Wetzel & S. Garrison-Kimmel 2020b), Rockstar (P. S. Behroozietal. 2012a), Consistent Trees (P. S. Behroozietal. 2012) i","AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS XZ led the analysis, developed the methodology, wrote","AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS XZ led the analysis, developed the methodology, wrote",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,193,148,False,False,0,148,True,4.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_012,12,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence between the local DM and stellar velocity distributions associated with mergers, both component-wise and for the full 3-dimensional distribution. Here, we presenta method for approximating the velocity distributionof accreted DM in major mergers using the kinematicsof its associated stellar counterpart. We convolve the stel lar velocity distribution witha Gaussian functionto bet ter match with the DM counterpart accreted from each merger and establisha scaling relationof the best fitpa rameters across the simulations asa functionof redshiftso thatit canbe generalizedto observation. Although accreted stars and DM are expectedto fol low similar phase-space distributions dueto their shared origin, in practicewe observed that the stellar veloc ity distributions are typically more concentrated than their DM counterparts. Fig. 7 shows the ratioof the stellar velocity dispersionto thatof the DM accreted from the same progenitor. For more recent mergers, the DM distributionis systematically broader than its stellar counterpart, while this discrepancy diminishesto ward higher accretion redshift. This trendis consistent with","3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence","3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_013,13,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 3.2.Untraceable mergers The untraceable mergers are definedin two categories first, the young untraceable, as mergers more massive than 109M⊙in halo mass but with little contributionto the local stellar mass, and second, the old untraceable, as those accreted earlier than redshift 3 whichis more ancient and phase-mixed. Their velocity distributions are shownin Fig. 10. The old untractable mergers canbe well approximated bya Gaussian distributionin allof their components except form12w which hasa non-trivialco-rotation. The young untraceable mergers, by contrast, deviate more strongly froma Gaussian form, consistent with their incomplete dynamical relaxation. Nevertheless, these distributions appear noisy and irregular dueto limited particle statis tics, making them difficultto model precisely. As shownin Table. S2, they only makeup sub 10% of the total mass. Sowe will assume themto behave the sameas the diffuse component which willbe discussedin the next section, and treat any other discrepancy asa sys tematic uncertainty.",3.2.Untraceable mergers The untraceable mergers are definedin two categories,3.2.Untraceable mergers The untraceable mergers are definedin two categories,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,205,157,False,False,0,157,False,3.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_014,14,"that constituent. In addition, the generalized Gaussian modelis ableto capture manyof the same features, including the lighter tails and broader central structure. One notableex ception, however, is the azimuthal component, where the full reconstruction performs significantly better thanthe generalized model. Thisis expected, as the SHM assumes velocity isotropy, whichis brokenin realistic halos dueto anisotropic infall and merger-inducedan gular momentum. In particular, major mergers can drive coherentco-rotationin the stellar and DM compo nents (J. I. Readetal. 2009; C. W. Purcelletal. 2009; A. Pillepichetal. 2014), leadingto deviations from the SHM prediction. These effects are more accurately cap turedby incorporating the stellar kinematicsof individ ual mergers into the reconstruction. More precisely, we compute the normalized EMDsbe tween the true DM speed distributions and three mod els: the SHM, the gSHM, and our full reconstructed model. These comparisons are shownin Fig. S6, allow inga direct assessmentof how well gSHM and there construction perform relativeto the SHM baseline. The results show that both gSHM and the reconstruction consistently outperform the SHM across all halos and ence between gSHM and the reconstruction themselves. This outcome reflectsa","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,1260,1440,False,3.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_015,15,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thankE.Y.Davies, D.Folsom, A.Hussein, M Lisanti, Z. Mezghanni, T. Schpigel, N. Starkman for from NSF, via CAREER award AST-2337864. L.N. is supportedby the Sloan Fellowship and the NSF CA NSF, via CAREER award AST-2045928. AA acknowl edges support from Gordon and Betty Moore founda tion.FIRE-2 simulations are publicly available (A. Wet zeletal. 2023, 2025) at http://flathub.flatironinstitute. org/fire. Additional FIRE simulation datais availableat https://fire.northwestern.edu/data. A public versionof the Gizmo codeis availableat http://www.tapir. caltech.edu/ ∼phopkins/Site/GIZMO.html. We gener ated simulations using: XSEDE, supportedby NSF grant ACI-1548562; Blue Waters, supportedby the NSF; Frontera allocations AST21010 and AST20016, sup portedby the NSF and TACC; Pleiades, via the NASA HEC program through the NAS Divisionat Ames Re search Center. We acknowledge the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC) at The Universityof Texasat Austin for providing computing resources that con tributedto our results.","ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thankE.Y.Davies, D.Folsom, A.Hussein, M","ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thankE.Y.Davies, D.Folsom, A.Hussein, M",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,194,149,False,False,0,149,False,3.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_016,16,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution Set the Nighton FIRE: Buildingan Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution Xiuyuan Zhang ,1Andreas Thoyas ,2Lina Necib ,1Andrew Wetzel ,3and Arpit Arora 1Departmentof Physics and Kavli Institute for Astrophysics and Space Research, MIT, Cambridge, MA 02139, USA 2Departmentof Physics, Northeastern University, Boston, MA 02115, USA 31Departmentof Physics & Astronomy, Universityof California, Davis, CA 95616, USA 4Departmentof Astronomy and DiRAC Institute, Universityof Washington, 3910 15th Ave NE, Seattle, WA, 98195, USA",Set the Nighton FIRE: Buildingan Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,Set the Nighton FIRE: Buildingan Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,108,83,False,False,0,83,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_017,17,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob jectsat various redshifts. Among these, the most signif icant contributorsto the local stellar population—those that deposita noticeable fractionof stars into the solar neighborhood—canbe tracked using their stellar coun terparts. Basedon this, we further classify the resolved mergers into three subcategories: (1) the top four merg ers that contribute the most stellar mass locally and canbe reliably tracked, whichwe call traceable mergers and are rankedby their contributionto the stellar con tentin the solar neighborhood, (2) young untraceable mergers that are recent and massive but lack clear stel lar signatures, and (3) old untraceable mergers that are too ancientor phase-mixedtobe associated with iden tifiable stellar debris. An illustrationof the fractional m12i m12f m12m m12w m12b m12c0.00.20.40.60.81.0FractionDiffuse Young untraceable Old untraceable Merger IMerger II Merger III Merger IVFigure 3. Fractional contributionsof the individual com ponentsto the local DM population. The diffuse compo nentis generally the dominant contributor across the m12 sample. By contrast, the DM contributions from the",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_018,18,"untraceable Old untraceable Merger IMerger II Merger III Merger IVFigure 3. Fractional contributionsof the individual com ponentsto the local DM population. The diffuse compo nentis generally the dominant contributor across the m12 sample. By contrast, the DM contributions from the top mergers—rankedby their associated stellar massin theso lar neighborhood—vary substantially, andin some cases canbe negligible relativeto the other components. The exact numerical valuesof all fractions are listedin Table. S2. contributionsof the individual componentsto the local DM populationis shownin Fig. 3.10. In this section, we describe how mergers are treated throughout the analysis. We beginby studying trace able mergersin Sec. 3.1. In Sec. 3.1.1, we introduce the statistical metric usedin this work, andin Secs. 3.1.2 and 3.1.3 we applyitto traceable mergersto quantify stellar–DM correlations. We then extend this analysisin Sec. 3.1.4, wherewe developan improved mapping from stellar kinematicsto the corresponding DM dis tributions for each progenitor. Finally, in Sec. 3.2, we discuss the treatmentof untraceable mergers and justify the assumptions adopted for their contributions.",3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob,3.DARK MATTER ACCRETED FROM MERGERS Mergers enter the host haloas discrete boundedob,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,212,163,False,False,140,303,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_019,19,"the phase mixingof earlier mergers. As shownin Fig. 5, the radial velocity componentsof the stellar and DM populations are generally simi lar regardlessof accretion timeor progenitor mass. All components exhibita dependenceon these quantities, but the vθandvϕcomponents showa stronger corre lation: DM and stellar distributions are more closely matched for earlier, lower-mass mergers than for more recent, massive ones. We note, however, that dueto the hierarchical growthof cold DM halos, earlier mergers also tendtobe less massive, as reflectedin the colorsof Fig. 5. This behavioris physically well motivated. As satel lites are accreted, they undergo violent relaxation and phase mixing predominantly along the radial direction, where the host potential varies most rapidly (D. Lynden Bell 1967; S. Tremaineetal. 1986; S. Tremaine 1999). The transverse velocity, by contrast, is tiedto the orbital angular momentum, whichis approximately conserved and therefore relaxes ona longer timescale. Differencesin the total speed distributions are consequently driven primarilyby discrepanciesin the transverse components. Moreover, because stars are concentratedat the cen terof their progenitor’s potential well anddo not extendas faras the DM, the outer DM is stripped first while the inner",3.1.2.Accretion History Equipped with the Earth Mover’s Distance (EMD),3.1.2.Accretion History Equipped with the Earth Mover’s Distance (EMD),,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_020,20,"Differencesin the total speed distributions are consequently driven primarilyby discrepanciesin the transverse components. Moreover, because stars are concentratedat the cen terof their progenitor’s potential well anddo not extendas faras the DM, the outer DM is stripped first while the inner stellar componentis disrupted later. The stel lar velocity distribution therefore lags behind the DM in termsof phase mixing: the radial componentsof both populations equilibrate relatively quickly, while the stel lar transverse component relaxes more slowly. Given sufficient time after infall, the accreted material becomesfully virialized and the stellar–DM correlation strength ens. Consistent with this picture, we find smaller EMD values for lower-mass mergers comparedto higher-mass ones accretedat the same time, because lower-mass mergers are more easily disrupted, whereas more mas sive subhalos retain extended DM envelopes that are stripped well before their inner stellar cores.",3.1.2.Accretion History Equipped with the Earth Mover’s Distance (EMD),3.1.2.Accretion History Equipped with the Earth Mover’s Distance (EMD),,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,176,135,False,False,420,555,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_021,21,"methods differ ina few respects. First, D. Fol sometal. (2025) applyan affine transformationto the stellar distribution (their equations 5 and 6), introduc inga Gaussian kernel ata later stage asa smoothing step, whereas our framework convolves the stellar dis tribution directly witha Gaussian kernel. Second, their treatment appliesa single velocity-dispersion boostav eraged across all components, while ours models each component separately and characterizes the stellar–DM mismatch asa functionof merger redshift. Third, their boost factors are reportedin physical unitsofkm/s, while ours are normalizedby the stellar MAD, making them dimensionless and therefore more readily compa rable across mergersof different mass scales.","3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence","3.1.4.Matching stellar velocity distribution with DM In previous sections, we quantified the correspondence",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,128,98,False,False,980,1078,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_022,22,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula tionis the diffuse component. This component includes both DM smoothly accreted onto the host halo andun resolved contributions from low-mass subhaloes below the resolution limitof the simulation (i.e. might not have enough star particlesto get correctly traced). For theLatte suite, we define the resolution threshold for resolved subhalosas 109M⊙in total mass. This thresh oldis scaled proportionally with the DM particle massin the lower- and higher-resolution versionsof each sim ulation, aswe discussin our resolutions tests (Sec. 6.2). The diffuse component constitutesa substantial frac tionof the local DM population—often exceeding the contribution from resolved mergers. The exact fraction varies significantly dependingon the specific accretion historyof the host halo and can range from approxi mately 40% toas highas 80% (see Tab.S2). As shownin Fig. 11, the diffuse component typically exhibitsa smoothly growing profile over time, with modest jumps that coincide with the accretion timesof major mergers. These featuresdo not solely imply significant misclassi ficationof merger-associated DM particlesas diffuseaswe testedin",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_023,23,"the host halo. As mergers are accreted, the assovr/vr 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(vr) [km/s]1 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 Old Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12cvr/vr 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(vr) [km/s]1 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 v/v 0.00.10.20.30.40.5f(v) [km/s]1 Young Untraceable Mergers Normal m12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c Figure 10. Velocity distributionsof untraceable mergers, separated into old and young populations. Old untraceable mergers exhibit velocity components that are approximately Gaussian, with the exceptionof m12w, which shows noticeableco-rotation. In contrast, younger untraceable mergers deviate significantly from Gaussian dueto incomplete dynamical relaxation. These distributions are noisy and irregular dueto limited particle statistics, making precise modeling challenging. As they contribute less than 10% of the total local DM mass (see Table S2), we approximate themas partof the diffuse component and treat any discrepanciesas systematic uncertainties. ciated time-dependent fluctuationsin the host gravita tional potential drive violent relaxation and redistribute the energies and orbital structureof the pre-existing dark matterM. Sparre & S. H. Hansen (2012); G. Ogiyaetal. (2016); N. W. Evansetal. (2019). Such episodes preferentially affect the radial degreeof freedom, broad ening and reshaping the vrdistribution relativeto the",4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,4.THE DIFFUSE COMPONENT The other major contributorto the local DM popula,,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,560,740,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_024,24,"Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution 5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon structing the local DM velocity distribution basedon the halo’s accretion history. As outlined above, we decom Fiducial Low Resolution High Resolution β α /vc β α /vc β α /vc r2.74±0.31 1.17 ±0.11 2.51 ±0.14 1.30 ±0.06 2.55 ±0.03 1.15 ±0.01 θ2.37±0.20 1.05 ±0.07 2.40 ±0.16 1.10 ±0.06 2.55 ±0.03 1.01 ±0.01 ϕ2.35±0.22 1.08 ±0.09 2.36 ±0.27 1.23 ±0.08 2.16 ±0.05 1.12 ±0.01 Table 3. Best-fit generalized Gaussian parameters for the diffuse DM velocity distribution. The location parameteris consistent with µ≃0 for all components, sowe report the remaining shape parameters (e.g., αandβ) for each velocity component. The same fittingis also appliedto low- and high-resolution simulations. 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 Redshift0.20.40.60.81.0Cumulative Diffuse Fractionm12i m12f m12w m12m m12b m12c Figure 11. Cumulative growthof the diffuse DM com ponent asa functionof accretion redshift, definedby the methodsin Sec. 2.2. The diffuse component can contribute between 40% and 80% of the local DM, dependingon the specific accretion history. The","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_025,25,"from direct observations. In practice, however, noneof them canbe directly observedsowe will haveto make the following assumption. The DM velocity dis tribution fifor eachof the merger canbe inferred from the stellar distributionas describedin Sec. 3.1.4, while the remaining material—including the diffuse compo nent and all untraceable mergers—is assumedto follow the generalized Gaussian form characterizedin Sec. 4. At this stage, the only remaining unknownin our reconstruction frameworkis the mass fractionof DM contributedby each mergerto the solar neighborhood. Observationally, itis extremely challengingto directly determine how much DM each progenitor depositslo cally. However, since DM and stars are accretedto gether—though with different spatial distributions and disruption timescales—itis plausible that the DM mass loss fraction relativeto the progenitoris correlated with the corresponding stellar mass loss fraction. Moreover, the total stellar massof each progenitor and its stellar debris are more accessible observationally, making the stellar mass loss fractiona potentially useful proxy for estimating the deposited DM. To investigate this, we compute the ratioof the DM loss fractionto the stellar loss fraction for each merger (Fig. 14). The ratio rises with increasing redshift before turning over","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,False,2.0
astrobench_e6b347fbf5,astrobench_e6b347fbf5_chunk_026,26,"direct assessmentof how well gSHM and there construction perform relativeto the SHM baseline. The results show that both gSHM and the reconstruction consistently outperform the SHM across all halos and ence between gSHM and the reconstruction themselves. This outcome reflectsa trade-off; while the reconstruc tion offers improved modelingof merger-induced DM kinematicsby incorporating boosted stellar velocity dis tributions, itis also more sensitiveto uncertaintiesin the inferred mass fractions. These introducea sourceof systematic error, which cannotbe resolved withouta more accurate understandingof how DM is deposited during tidal disruption.","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon","5.VELOCITY RECONSTRUCTION In this section, we presenta framework for recon",,,,,Set the Night on FIRE: Building an Empirical Local Dark Matter Velocity Distribution,"Xiuyuan Zhang, Andreas Thoyas, Lina Necib, Andrew Wetzel, Arpit Arora",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25783v1,2603.25783,galactic_dynamics,111,85,False,False,1400,1485,False,2.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_000,0,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk galaxies, whilea key morphological feature for galaxy classifica tion, is similarly significant for driving galaxy evolution. Bars have beendemonstratedtoaffecthost-galaxyprocessesonallscales:from star formationon molecular-cloud scales (10−100pc; e.g. Kunoetal. 2000; Dobbsetal. 2006, 2014; Nilipouretal. 2024; Maedaetal. 2025) to galaxy-wide dynamics (∼1000pc; e.g. Reganetal. 1999; O’Neill & Dubinski 2003; Emsellemetal. 2006; Ilesetal. 2024;Géronetal.2024;Rutherfordetal.2025);includingdisk–halo and disk–bulgeco-evolution (e.g. Athanassoula 2002; Valenzuela & Klypin 2003; Kormendy & Kennicutt 2004; Jogeeetal. 2005; Athanassoulaetal. 2013; Kruketal. 2018), angular momentum distribution (e.g. Weinberg 1985; Athanassoula 2005; Romeoetal. 2023), Active Galactic Nuclei (AGN) (e.g. Shlosmanetal. 1989; Alonsoetal.2014;Garlandetal.2023),Inter-StellarMedium(ISM) structure (Friedli & Benz 1993; Lindblad & Lindblad 1994; Pettitt etal.2020;Katsiolietal.2026),gasdepletionandquenching(Mas tersetal. 2012; Spinosoetal. 2017; Fraser-McKelvieetal. 2020; Géronetal. 2021), for example. Accounting forupto∼75% of nearbydiskgalaxies(e.g.Schinnereretal.2002;Aguerrietal.2009; Nair&Abraham2010;Saha&Elmegreen2018),anincreasingnum ★E-mail: elizabeth.iles@sydney.edu.au)berofgalaxiesathighredshift(e.g.Guoetal.2023;Costantinetal. 2023; Liangetal. 2024; Le Conteetal. 2024, 2025), and even the Milky Way (e.g. Blitz & Spergel 1991; Nakadaetal. 1991; Paczyn skietal.1994;Zhaoetal.1994;Bland-Hawthorn&Gerhard2016), these features are well-studied and yet, despite their ubiquity and impact, we still lacka distinct, universally-applicable method usedto define the spatial extentof the “bar” in",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_001,1,"2025), and even the Milky Way (e.g. Blitz & Spergel 1991; Nakadaetal. 1991; Paczyn skietal.1994;Zhaoetal.1994;Bland-Hawthorn&Gerhard2016), these features are well-studied and yet, despite their ubiquity and impact, we still lacka distinct, universally-applicable method usedto define the spatial extentof the “bar” in these galaxies (see, for recent example: Dehnenetal. 2023; Luceyetal. 2023; Petersenetal. 2024; Ilesetal. 2025), let aloneto identify whetheranin dividual star isa memberof the baror just passing through. For example,Fourieranalysis(e.g.Elmegreen&Elmegreen1985;Ohtaetal. 1990; Aguerrietal. 1998; Garcia-Gómezetal. 2017; Pettitt & Wadsley 2018; Frosstetal. 2025) has long been usedto identify bars, particularlyin simulations, while observational studies have traditionally reliedon isophotal ellipse fitting (e.g. Abrahametal. 1999;Laineetal.2002;Erwin2005;Gadotti&deSouza2006;Mari nova & Jogee 2007; Menendez-Delmestreetal. 2007; Consolandi 2016; Jiangetal. 2018) or photometric decomposition (e.g. Reeseetal. 2007; Gadotti 2008; Durbalaetal. 2008, 2009; Weinzirletal. 2009; Kruketal. 2018; Fraser-McKelvieetal. 2025). Visual clas sification (e.g. Nair & Abraham 2010; Hoyleetal. 2011; Masters etal.2011,2012;Melvinetal.2014;Kruketal.2018;Mastersetal. 2021; Géronetal. 2021) remains common, as bars are inherently visual features, but thisis increasingly impractical for large surveys andis inherently subjectto significant person-to-person variability (e.g. Lahavetal. 1995; Heidletal. 2013; Qianetal. 2022; Ilesetal. 2025). Crowdsourcing (e.g. Lahavetal. 1995; Fortsonetal. 2E.J. Ilesetal. 2018;",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_002,2,"Géronetal. 2021) remains common, as bars are inherently visual features, but thisis increasingly impractical for large surveys andis inherently subjectto significant person-to-person variability (e.g. Lahavetal. 1995; Heidletal. 2013; Qianetal. 2022; Ilesetal. 2025). Crowdsourcing (e.g. Lahavetal. 1995; Fortsonetal. 2E.J. Ilesetal. 2018; Fosteretal. 2025) can reduce this variation but canbe simi larlyinefficientandsubjectiveifnotappliedcarefully.Morerecently, automated bar-detection methods using non-parametric deep learn ing, via classification, regression, and segmentation (e.g. Abrahametal. 2018; Cavanagh & Bekki 2020; Fluke & Jacobs 2020; Ca vanaghetal. 2022; Huertas-Company & Lanusse 2023; Cavanaghetal. 2024), have become increasingly prevalent. However, since there areso many feasible methodsto identify the barin galaxies, itis necessaryto selecta ‘best’ method for each study. This presentsa fundamental problem: do anyof these methods consistently recover the same structure across all barred galaxies? The definition ofa bar, or barred region, is fundamentalto many studiesof host-galaxy properties, suchas gas flowor star formation along the bar (e.g. Downesetal. 1996; Shethetal. 2002; Momose etal.2010;Casasolaetal.2011;Watanabeetal.2019;Querejetaetal. 2021;Ilesetal.2022),aswellascorrelationsbetweenbarlengthand large-scale galactic properties, suchas longer barsin more massive galaxies(e.g.Kormendy1979;Erwin2005;Díaz-Garcíaetal.2016), or shorter barsin late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985;Combes&Elmegreen1993;Erwin2005;Aguerrietal.2009; Erwin2019)andwhetherthismayberedshiftdependent(e.g.Sheth etal.2008;Kimetal.2021).Ifthedefinitionofthebardiffersbetween methods,thephysicaltrendsweobservemaybesignificantlyeffectedby this systematic bias. So, despite our",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_003,3,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using 𝑁−body SPH (smoothed particle hydrodynamics) in the formofan isolated disk galaxy simulation (IsoB) from Ilesetal. (2022). To test bar-finding ona star-by-star basis, simulations with high tem poral and spatial resolution were necessarytobe ableto trace bar Action Bar Classification3 evolutionandstellarorbits.TheIsoBsimulationhasastellarparticle mass resolution of1044M ⊙for simulation-formed stars, a stellar softening lengthof 50pc anda snapshot for every 10Myrof galaxy evolution, whichis approximately equivalentto the star formation timescale (𝑡 SF=10Myr). This disk was evolved withgasoline2 (Wadsleyetal. 2017), comprisinga dark matter component, a small stellarbulge,aninitialstellardiskandagasdiskcomponentofparti clesrespectively,followingthestandardsub-gridprescriptions(Katzetal. 1996; Stinsonetal. 2006); with super-bubble feedback (Kelleretal. 2014), UV and photoelectric heating, as wellas metal coolingin the form ofa tabulated cooling function (Shenetal. 2010), re covering the two-phase ISM (Wolfireetal. 2003) froman initially isothermal (104K) gas profile. Star formation was prescribed bya standard temperature (300K) and density (100atoms/cc) threshold, aswellasaconvergentflowrequirementwithanefficiencyof∼10% andaChabrier(2003)initialmassfunction(IMF).Theinitialcondi tionwasoriginallytailoredtoobservationsofnearby,resolvedgalaxy ngc4303via the rotation curve, gas and stellar mass fractions (see Ilesetal. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved fora relatively short period of1Gyr, however, thisis",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_004,4,"formation was prescribed bya standard temperature (300K) and density (100atoms/cc) threshold, aswellasaconvergentflowrequirementwithanefficiencyof∼10% andaChabrier(2003)initialmassfunction(IMF).Theinitialcondi tionwasoriginallytailoredtoobservationsofnearby,resolvedgalaxy ngc4303via the rotation curve, gas and stellar mass fractions (see Ilesetal. 2022, for more details). The simulation was evolved fora relatively short period of1Gyr, however, thisis sufficientto covera period pre-bar formation, the initial bar formation, anda shortpe riod post-bar formationto capture action propertiesin eachof these regimes.LatersnapshotsoftheIsoBdiskcanbeconsideredaclassic example ofa barred galaxy, however, automated bar identification methodsdonotconsistentlyproducerealisticbarparametersforthis diskin every snapshot. Thus, we consider this simulationa good elementary demonstrationof the proposed method, as there are suf ficient snapshots with automated bar classification for comparison but automation can struggle with this galaxy. Additionally, we have recentlycompletedvisualclassificationforeachsnapshotoftheIsoB simulation fora study into visual bar classification (Ilesetal. 2025) which, despite some limitations, can alsobe used asa comparison for the bar parametersof this diskas measured ‘by-eye’.",2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,2.1 The Test Simulations We demonstrate the practicalityof the proposed method using,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,172,132,False,False,140,272,True,5.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_005,5,"formation along the bar (e.g. Downesetal. 1996; Shethetal. 2002; Momose etal.2010;Casasolaetal.2011;Watanabeetal.2019;Querejetaetal. 2021;Ilesetal.2022),aswellascorrelationsbetweenbarlengthand large-scale galactic properties, suchas longer barsin more massive galaxies(e.g.Kormendy1979;Erwin2005;Díaz-Garcíaetal.2016), or shorter barsin late-type galaxies (e.g Elmegreen & Elmegreen 1985;Combes&Elmegreen1993;Erwin2005;Aguerrietal.2009; Erwin2019)andwhetherthismayberedshiftdependent(e.g.Sheth etal.2008;Kimetal.2021).Ifthedefinitionofthebardiffersbetween methods,thephysicaltrendsweobservemaybesignificantlyeffectedby this systematic bias. So, despite our abilityto define the barin manyways,definingthebarinawaythatisdistinct,repeatable,and applicableacrossbarredgalaxiesmuststillbeapriorityforthefield. Itis onlyin this way thatwe can most effectively approacha unified understandingofbarstructure,galaxyformation,andtheroleofbarsin driving galaxy evolution.",1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,1.1 Background The ubiquitous bar-like structure presentin the centreof many disk,,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-26T02:45:29Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.24952v1,2603.24952,galactic_dynamics,78,60,False,False,420,480,True,4.0
astrobench_1e3dee4300,astrobench_1e3dee4300_chunk_006,6,"Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation 1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies, particularly aimedat identifying barsin simulations with high res olution capturing complexor non-standard systems, suchas double bars, nuclear disks, or bars distortedby interactions, with the added capacityto track time evolutionon galactic scales. Further, this pro cessis designedbe ableto determine, not only the spatial extentof the bar but also, the membershipof individual starsto the bar, dis tinguishing starson bar-supporting orbits, or orbits affectedby the bar, from those merely passing through. This capability will enable theassessmentofimportantbar-drivenprocesses,includingstarfor mation, radial migration and chemical mixing, ona more precise, star-by-star basis, at leastin simulations. Thebasisofthismethodistoexploittheintrinsicpropertiesofthe angle–actionvariables(𝜃 𝑖,𝐽𝑖)indifferentregionsofthegalacticpo tential. Action-space, describedby these angle-action variables, has long been usedto probe many propertiesof the Milky Way through Galacticdynamics(e.g.Lynden-Bell&Kalnajs1972;Kalnajs1977; Arnold 1978; Martinetetal. 1981; Rauch & Tremaine 1996; Wein berg2001;Sellwood2012).Thisisprimarilyduetotheconvenienceof the action integrals, asa setof adiabatic invariant quantities for any quasi-periodic orbit ina steady, or slowly varying, potential (see Binney & Tremaine 2008, fora more specific detail). Actions have been instrumentalin modelling phase-mixed populations (e.g. Tremaine 1999; Antojaetal. 2018; Ting & Rix","1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies,","1.2 Dynamical Actions We proposea different approachto classifying the barin galaxies,",,,,,Bar Properties and Star-by-Star Bar Membership via Action Conservation,"Elizabeth J. Iles, Finn A. Pal, Joss Bland-Hawthorn, Ken Freeman",astro-ph.GA,2026-