astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_023,23,"Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding 5.2.Exploring individualT fitcurve features with toy models Our basic setup for these toy models isa 1D systemof 105test particles ina specified potential, meant torepresenta single (θ ϕ, Jϕ) bin. These particles are ini tializedinanln cosh potential (the sameas the vertical potentialof theN-body simulation) with scale heightzh and are initiallyin equilibrium around thez= 0 mid plane. In each variationof this toy model, we imparta velocity kickon these particles equalto the velocity dispersionσ z. We then allow the particlesto evolve but change the potentialin prescribed ways correspondingto different physical mechanismsin orderto recreate the various featuresin Fig. 5. Specifically, we explore effectsof the disk midplane reformingasit relaxes froma per turbation (Section 5.2.1); its vertical structure evolving, 0 20 40 60 80 100 Toy Model Time [ zh/σz]020406080100Tfit[zh/σz]tmix= 0zh/σz tmix= 5zh/σz tmix= 25zh/σz tmix= 100zh/σz Figure 8.The resultsof running our first 1-D dimensionless toy model (see Section 5.2.1) with differentt mixvalues, which specifies the characteristic timescaleon which the original disk midplane repopulates itself dueto phase-mixing and relaxation. Both time axes arein dimensionless unitsof",5.2.Exploring individualT fitcurve features with toy models,5.2.Exploring individualT fitcurve features with toy models,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,4.5
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_024,24,"arough estimateofJ zand the position along the ellipse canbe usedto indicateθ z(e.g. Price-Whelanetal. 2021). While this comparisonis conceptually sim ple, the italicized differences make direct dynamicalin terpretationsof distributionsin (z, vz) space challeng ing. First, the orbitsin (z, vz) arein general not closed, so the pathis not strictly repeated. Second, the progres sionin time along that pathis non-uniform. Third, the shapeof the path, while simple, deviates significantly from something that canbe described with compact for mulae. Finally, orbitsin this coordinate system typically overlap with each other, unless restrictingto those with zero radial action (or, equivalently, eccentricity). Com bined, these leadto distributions (and phase spirals) in (z, vz) that are, at certain radii, non-circular (i.e. more “angular” or pointy) and non-trivially represented. Distributionsin (z, vz) can directlybe comparedto thosein (θ z, Jz) by plotting the angle-action variablesin polar coordinates (√Jzcosθ z,√Jzsinθ z). In this space, star orbits follow (exact and closed) circlesof radius√Jz, with phases increasing steadily from positionθ z,0 with timetasθ z= Ω zt+θ z,0. Aftera perturbation, these distributions spiral outward steadilyas the stars within them followa progressionof circular paths. For","3.1.Choiceof coordinate systems Throughout our analysis, we use angle-action rather","3.1.Choiceof coordinate systems Throughout our analysis, we use angle-action rather",,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,4.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_025,25,"In this space, star orbits follow (exact and closed) circlesof radius√Jz, with phases increasing steadily from positionθ z,0 with timetasθ z= Ω zt+θ z,0. Aftera perturbation, these distributions spiral outward steadilyas the stars within them followa progressionof circular paths. For reference, the actionsJ ϕandJ zare relatedto guiding radiiR Gand maximum vertical oscillationam plitudez maxvia RG≡Jϕ vcirc(Jϕ)≃8.2 kpcJϕ 1900 kpckm/s230 km/s vcirc(1) zmax≃2Jzvz,max (Jz)≃300 pc2Jz 9 kpckm/s30 km/svz,max(2) In these equations,v circ(Jϕ) is the circular velocity ofa star witha givenJ ϕ,vz,max (Jz) is the maximum vertical velocity ofa star witha givenJz, and the numberson the right are approximations for the Solar Neighborhood.","3.1.Choiceof coordinate systems Throughout our analysis, we use angle-action rather","3.1.Choiceof coordinate systems Throughout our analysis, we use angle-action rather",,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,132,101,False,True,280,381,True,4.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_026,26,"our estimate for the local acceleration actually exceeds the full disk accelerationas our assumptionofan infinitely extended, high-surface density disk becomes unrealistic (see Section 5.1).Right:The sameas the middle panel but for the MW. the full disk accelerationin the inner diskas our assump tionofan infinitely extended, high-surface density disk becomes unrealistic. Hence, in the inner diskwe expect the acceleration fieldtobe significantly perturbedif the disk itselfis perturbed. Consistent with this, in ourN-body resultswe find that the phase spiralsin these regions have large winding delays. We explore one physical mechanism that might cause this delayin Section 5.2.1.","results In contrast, local patchesin the middle and inner disk","results In contrast, local patchesin the middle and inner disk",,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,121,93,False,False,280,373,True,4.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_027,27,"are those where thede rivedT fitvalues are less trustworthy. For theN-body casewe determine the trustworthinessof aT fitcalcu lation basedon how monotonicθ z,max (Jz) is. We note that thisis quitea conservative cut, sowe chooseto still show the derivedT fitvalues for these regions. Basedon our analysis, we can also offera suggestion for howto find the true winding time from our obser vational results. OurN-body simulation clearly demon strates that, fora disk where phase spirals are induced bya satellite,T fitcalculated from phase spiralsin the outer disk are more accurate than those near the So lar Neighborhoodorin the inner disk. Our toy models, where the perturbationis more generic, suggest that thisis likelytobe true for other typesof perturbationsas well. This gives usa clear strategy for finding the true perturbation time responsible for the current phase spi ralsin the MW: trust the winding timesin the outer disk more than thosein the inner disk. Applying thisto our resultsin the left panelof Fig. 10, we suggest that, if the MW phase phase spirals sharea common origin, the perturbation which caused them likely occurred nearly","6.1.Connecting simulation resultsto the Milky Way In the left and right panelsof Fig.7, we make the same","6.1.Connecting simulation resultsto the Milky Way In the left and right panelsof Fig.7, we make the same",,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,229,176,False,False,280,456,True,4.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_028,28,"the disk crossing time ofa Sgr-like satellite. In fact, the winding time calculations wereso precise thatwe could seea dipole inT fitbasedon whether the region was more perturbed beforeor after the satellite passed through the disk (see Fig. 4). We performed this analysison the test particle casetoen sure that both our BFE reconstructions and winding time derivations were working accurately before apply ing bothto the self-consistent simulation. In theN-body simulation, the same analysis revealed significant deviations forT fitderived from the phase spirals comparedto our expectations dueto phase mixing alone. These deviations were particularly pro nouncedin the inner galaxy where the disk dominates the gravitational potential. We used toy modelsto explore the extentto which the expected evolutionof the disk potential (e.g. oscillationsof the midplane, breathing modes and disk thickening) could explain the deviations inT fitthatwe observed. We found that the models provided useful intuition for some, but not all, of the behaviorwe could see.",7.1.Summary The dynamicsof phase spiral formation and evolution,7.1.Summary The dynamicsof phase spiral formation and evolution,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,198,152,False,False,140,292,True,4.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_029,29,"gies and winding times can potentiallybe usedto: dis tinguish formation scenarios (e.g. Sgr, subhalos, darkmatter halo distortions); and measure physical quanti ties (e.g. orbitof Sgr, frequencyof subhalo interactions, scaleof dark matter halo distortion). We conclude that, when using winding times for physical interpretations: (i) winding times derived from MW phase spirals shouldbe considered lower limits; and (ii) the winding timesin the outer disk are likelytobe more accurate than thosein the inner diskor the Solar Neighborhood.","7.2.Conclusions Our results confirm prior work, which argued that the","7.2.Conclusions Our results confirm prior work, which argued that the",,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,98,75,False,False,280,355,True,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_030,30,"Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding Phase spirals across galactic disksI: Exploring dynamical influenceson winding Kiyan Tavangar ,1KathrynV. Johnston ,1JasonA. S. Hunt ,2Axel Widmark ,1, 3Chris Hamilton ,4, 5 MichaelS. Petersen ,6and MartinD. Weinberg 1Departmentof Astronomy, Columbia University, New York, NY 10027, USA 2Schoolof Mathematics & Physics, Universityof Surrey, Stag Hill, Guildford, GU2 7XH, UK 3Stockholm University and The Oskar Klein Centre for Cosmoparticle Physics, Alba Nova, 10691 Stockholm, Sweden 4Schoolof Natural Sciences, Institute for Advanced Study, Princeton, NJ 08540, USA 5Departmentof Astrophysical Sciences, 4 Ivy Lane, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ 08544, USA 6Institute for Astronomy, Universityof Edinburgh, Royal Observatory, Blackford Hill, Edinburgh EH9 3HJ, UK 7Departmentof Astronomy, Universityof Massachusettsat Amherst, 710 N. Pleasant St., Amherst, MA 01003",Phase spirals across galactic disksI: Exploring dynamical influenceson winding,Phase spirals across galactic disksI: Exploring dynamical influenceson winding,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,160,123,False,False,0,123,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_031,31,"outto greater Galac tocentric radii. Despite the differencein potentials, we believeitis still appropriateto compare these two sim ulationsto analyze how incorporatingN-body interac tions affects phase spiral formation and evolution. The test particle case providesa clear demonstrationof the validityof our intuition for the evolutionof the phase spiral dueto phase-mixing alone afteran isolated satel lite interaction. Itis true that the precise shapeof the equilibrium potential will havea quantitative influenceon the rateof phase spiral winding, and that different interactions (froma single satelliteor other perturba tions) will provide different initial conditions that insti gate that evolution. However, our purposeisto study the deviationof theN-body case from our expectations for phase-mixing, whichwe cando qualitatively, withouta direct quantitative comparisonof the simulations.",2.2.Self-consistentN-body simulation 4Tavangaretal,2.2.Self-consistentN-body simulation 4Tavangaretal,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,149,114,False,False,560,674,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_032,32,"Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding 3.1.2.Binning particles witha common history Workingin angle-action space rather than physical space also ensureswe can group stars together that have similar orbital frequencies and, likely, common histories. Consider two stars that areat some pointin close phys ical proximity (i.e. nearbyin (x, y) coordinates), but with oneat its orbital apocenter and oneat pericenter. The starat pericenter (apocenter) will, on average, orbit the galaxy ata smaller (larger) radius, meaningit will havea shorter (longer) orbital period. Therefore, these two stars will not have been closeto one another for the overwhelming majorityof their orbital histories. Asa consequence, they will experience different effects from both the underlying galactic potential and any pertur bative forces. Ifwe instead select two disk stars for their proximityin angle-action space, with small (δθ ϕ, δJϕ)3 and differencesin frequenciesδΩ ϕ. Ina static, axisym metric potential,δJ ϕis conserved for all time whileδθ ϕ will grow only very slowly,≃δΩ ϕt. These stars willex perience similar histories andbe sensitiveto the same perturbations. (See Huntetal. 2020, fora more com plete discussion). With these considerationsin",3.1.2.Binning particles witha common history Workingin angle-action space rather than physical,3.1.2.Binning particles witha common history Workingin angle-action space rather than physical,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_033,33,"Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding 3.2.2.Deriving winding times One descriptorof the morphology ofa spiralis the tightnessof the winding, which canbe quantifiedby its pitch angle,ψ. This canbe translated toa quantityof relevance for dynamics and historyby calculating the winding time (whichwe will callT fitfollowing the nota tionin Asano & Antoja 2025), or the timeit would take for the spiralto attain this pitch angle, starting froman asymmetric distribution and evolvingin the absenceof self gravity through phase-mixing alone. Our primary goalin this paperisto understand the effectivenessof usingT fitto recover the timeat which the perturbation which inciteda spiral actually occurred. In this section and for the remainderof this work, we will focuson the one-armed phase space spirals. Thisis both because they are the dominant spiralsin our simulations and because Widrow (2023) showed that their evolutionin the presenceof self-gravityis simpler than thatof the two-armed spirals, whichwe leaveto future work. Our method for derivingT fitfora given spiralis very similarto that adoptedby previous works (e.g. Darragh Fordetal. 2023; Asano & Antoja 2025), with the key difference being thatwe are working directly using",3.2.2.Deriving winding times One descriptorof the morphology ofa spiralis the,3.2.2.Deriving winding times One descriptorof the morphology ofa spiralis the,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_034,34,"Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding 4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti cle simulation asa sanity checkto ensure ourT fitcal culation from the BFE reconstructions gives reasonable results. As describedin Section 2.1 these phase spirals form following the disk crossing ofa satellite≃15 kpc from the galaxy’s center. In the left gridof Fig. 3, we show the resulting one-armed phase spiral reconstruc tion for 15 example spiralsat three differentJ ϕand five different times. As expected, we find that the phase spi rals windup over time until they becomeso wound that natural processof phase mixingas the system returns toa quasi-equilibrium state. Using the method describedin Section 3.2.2, wede rive the perturbation time from each spiralin the disk. We show the resultsin Fig. 4, which visualizes themin two ways. In the left panel, we showT fitfor the spiralin each region approximately 0.4 Gyr after the satel lite disk crossing. The colorbar shows the winding time, withT fitvalues indicating perturbation times slightly before (after) the disk crossing shownin blue (red). We",4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti,4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_035,35,"the left panel, we showT fitfor the spiralin each region approximately 0.4 Gyr after the satel lite disk crossing. The colorbar shows the winding time, withT fitvalues indicating perturbation times slightly before (after) the disk crossing shownin blue (red). We indicate this winding time bothin Gyr andin units oftpassage ≡rsat/vz,satwith bothr satandvz,sattakenat the disk crossing time. We derive the expectedT fit valueof≃0.4 Gyrto within 50 Myr for nearly every regionin the disk. However, we notice that while the perturbation times are constrained toa narrow range, they appeartobe bimodal, with very fewof the regionshavingT fitof exactly 0.4 Gyr. Instead, they are mostly slightly earlieror later and regions with similar derived times seemto fall along diagonal ridges. Given that regionsat differentJ ϕvalues have different orbitalpe riods and therefore different orbital frequencies, we can apply the same physicsto these diagonal ridgesas for the phase spiral winding time calculations. In other words, we can rewind the disk spiralin the same waywe did the phase spiral. Wedo thisto create the second and third panelsof Fig. 4, wherewe seea bimodality much more clearly and observe that",4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti,4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_036,36,"they are closertoit. On the other hand, regions moving away from the impact point experiencea larger force before the disk crossing. This leads toa slight leading/trailing offsetin the timesat which regionsof the disk ahead/behind the satellite’s disk crossing experience the interaction. In our test particle simulation, stars rotate counter clockwise and the satellite goes from negativeto pos itivezvalues. The dipolewe see matches this physical picture. Finally, in the left panelsof Fig. 5, we plot the 50 Myr moving averageof the medianT fitin eachJ ϕannulusat each timestepof the simulation, along with the residual relativeto the expected value. As anticipated for this simulation, we find that the pure phase mixing theory describes the post perturbation stateof one-armed phase spirals throughout the disk very well4. 4.2.N-body phase spiral winding We now analyze how phase spiral winding differs ina self-consistent simulation. Sincewe are tryingto isolate this effect, we focuson the sectionof theN-body simula tion most similarto the one analyzedin the test particle case: the period between the first and second passagesof the satellite through the disk. This allowsusto examine 4The discontinuitiesat later times for",4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti,4.1.Test particle phase spiral winding We first examine the phase spiralsin the test parti,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,420,600,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_037,37,"Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding IN THEN-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION 5To avoid including regions where theT fitcalculation clearly failed, we only consider regions withT fitvalues between 0 and 2 times the value expected from phase mixing theory.Prior workon the evolutionof phase spirals has shown that accounting for self-gravity – eitherby using self consistent simulationsor mimickingN-body interactions some other way – leadsto winding delays (Darling & Widrow 2019a,b, 2021; Bennett & Bovy 2021; Widrow 2023; Bland-Hawthorn & Tepper-Garc´ ıa 2021; Darling & Widrow 2024; Asano & Antoja 2025). The resultsin this paper more explicitly demonstrate that phase spirals inN-body simulations exhibit (i) winding delays, (ii) winding slowdowns, and (iii) oscillationsin the wind ing rates, as shown inT fitcurvesin the right panelof Fig. 5. Allof these effects vary asa function ofJ ϕ. In this section, we explore just one aspectof the dynamics influencing phase-spiral evolution — namely, howan evolving galactic disk can affect the winding ofa groupof particles, traveling togetherin oneof our (θϕ, Jϕ)-bins. In Section 5.1, we first examine theJ ϕ dependenceof the natureof windingby consideringlo",IN THEN-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION 5To avoid including regions where theT fitcalculation clearly,IN THEN-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION 5To avoid including regions where theT fitcalculation clearly,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_eb380fd57f,astrobench_eb380fd57f_chunk_038,38,"just one aspectof the dynamics influencing phase-spiral evolution — namely, howan evolving galactic disk can affect the winding ofa groupof particles, traveling togetherin oneof our (θϕ, Jϕ)-bins. In Section 5.1, we first examine theJ ϕ dependenceof the natureof windingby consideringlo cal and global contributionsto particle accelerationsin different regionsof the disk; in Section 5.2, wegoonto use toy modelsto build intuition for how the evolving disk potential might delay, slow-downor cause oscilla tionsin the winding rates andin Section 5.3 we assess how muchof whatwe have observedin our models canbe attributedto the evolving disk alone.",IN THEN-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION 5To avoid including regions where theT fitcalculation clearly,IN THEN-BODY WINDING EVOLUTION 5To avoid including regions where theT fitcalculation clearly,,,,,Phase spirals across galactic disks I: Exploring dynamical influences on winding,"Kiyan Tavangar, Kathryn V. Johnston, Jason A. S. Hunt, Axel Widmark, Chris Hamilton, Michael S. Petersen, Martin D. Weinberg",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-24T18:00:00Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23588v1,2603.23588,galactic_dynamics,120,92,False,False,140,232,False,3.0
