astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_000,0,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 3.1.1 The Milky Way To model the MW dark matter halowe usea Navarro-Frenk-White (NFW, Navarroetal. 1996, 1997) dark matter halo density profile described by𝑀 200and𝑐 200; see Table. 1 for the mass prior range considered. These quantities are defined bya sphere enclosingan overdensity thatis 200 times the critical densityof the Universe, 𝜌crit=3𝐻2 0/8𝜋𝐺, as denotedby the ‘200’ subscript, where Hub ble parameter,𝐻 0, is takento be67.6 kms−1Mpc−1using thede faultcosmologyinastropy(AstropyCollaborationetal.2013).We constrain the normalisationof the halo mass profile such that the circular velocityat the solar positionis approximately235 kms−1 (e.g., matching constraints from McMillan 2017, within the associ ated uncertainty).1The MW stellar components are modelled using asphericalbulgewithatotalmass1.2×1010M⊙,andanexponential stellar disc witha total mass5×1010M⊙. The stellar distributions remain fixed taking the values suggestedby McMillan (2017).",3.1.1 The Milky Way To model the MW dark matter halowe usea Navarro-Frenk-White,3.1.1 The Milky Way To model the MW dark matter halowe usea Navarro-Frenk-White,,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,176,135,False,True,0,135,True,5.0
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_001,1,"the reflex motion–Top panel: A stereographic projection centredon the south galactic pole showing the apex directionof the travel velocity,(𝑙 apex, 𝑏apex). The red posterior contours are placedat the40th,70th, and90thquantiles, correspondingto Gaussian-equivalent levels of0.5𝜎,1𝜎, and1.5𝜎. The posterior distributionis conditionedon the meananddispersion velocity data after removing spurious data points. The values from existing literature are shownas markers (Vasilievetal. 2021; Yaaqibetal. 2024; Chandraetal. 2025b; Byströmetal. 2025, in yellow using Sgr data, orange using SDSS data, red using H3+SEGUE+MagE data, and dark blue using DESI data, respectively). Additionally, we show the apex directionof the fiducial𝑁-body simulationin Garavito-Camargoetal. (2019) as the light blue pentagon. The past LMC orbitis calculated using the best-fit values from the inference with the present-day location denotedby the black star. Lower panel: A comparisonof the magnitudeof the travel velocity,𝑣 travel, from the same literature values and our posterior median and1𝜎percentilesas the red line and shaded region. The prior1𝜎confidence intervalis shownas the grey dashed lines.","6.1.2 The LMC Similarly, we place our LMC mass resultsin context with other pre","6.1.2 The LMC Similarly, we place our LMC mass resultsin context with other pre",,,,,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars","Richard A. N. Brooks, Jason L. Sanders, Adam M. Dillamore, Nicolás Garavito-Camargo, Vedant Chandra, Adrian M. Price-Whelan, Phillip Cargile",astro-ph.GA,2026-03-09T18:00:04Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08788v1,2603.08788,galactic_dynamics,199,153,False,False,280,433,True,5.0
astrobench_b7b4f9944a,astrobench_b7b4f9944a_chunk_002,2,"Quantifying the Milky Way, LMC and their interaction using all-sky kinematics of outer halo stars 6.2 Caveats and Limitations OurapproachofusingrigidmodelsfortheMW–LMCsystemscomes with various caveats. Someof the limitationsof rigid modelsin clude: assuming that the MW isin equilibrium prior the LMC’s gainedby either halo, not accounting for halo triaxiality, and usingasimpleChandrasekharprescriptionfordynamicalfriction(seedis cussionsectionsinBrooksetal.2026,2025).Ideally,onewouldusea suiteof𝑁-bodyMW–LMCsimulationsthatcannaturallyaccountfor mostof the above limitations. Moreover,𝑁-body simulations would allowtheMWdisctomoveseparatelyfromitsdarkmatterhalo,bet termodellingtheinducedreflexmotionfromtheLMC(Yaaqibetal. Quantifying the MW and LMC with SBI11 020406080vtravel [kms−1]H3+/angbracketleftv/angbracketright∗,/angbracketleftσv/angbracketright∗ Chandra+25 −100−50050100lapex[deg] 40 60 80 100 120 rgal[kpc]−80−70−60−50−40bapex[deg]Simulation 1 σ Figure6.Radialvariationofthereflexmotion:Theradialvariationofthe magnitude,𝑣 travel, and the apex direction,(𝑙 apex, 𝑏apex), of the reflex motion posteriors.Weshowthemedianastheredlineandthe1𝜎confidenceinterval astheshadedband.Thesimulationprior1𝜎confidenceintervalisshownas the grey shaded band. The red data point represents the result from Chandra etal.(2025b)evaluatedat100 kpcusingthesameH3+SEGUE+MagEdataset but distinct methodology. 2025). However, aswe have shown, even state-of-the-art𝑁-body simulations arein tension with the measured reflex motion parame ters;seeFig.5.Infuturestudies,usingasuiteof𝑁-bodysimulations (e.g., Shengetal. 2025) to trainan SBI architectureto estimate the posteriordistributionofe.g.,thereflexmotionparameters,willhigh lightanybiasesintheinferenceoftheseparameterwhenusingrigid modelsto train similar SBI networks. Oneof the other clear limitationsof our rigid MW–LMC model set-upistheinabilitytoexplorelargeregionsofsimulationparame ter spacein