astrobench_c59a82f3b2,astrobench_c59a82f3b2_chunk_036,36,"valuesof Vφare plotted with red dots; they closely align the analytic rotation curve, showing deviations only near the bordersof the reso nances. The main effectsof the perturbation observedin Fig. 5 are the sharp increasesin the gradual changeof Vφrela tive toR. They are originatedby Lindblad resonances2 (LRs), which excite the Vφ–amplitudes. The main LRs are indicatedby vertical dashed lines and are labeledin Fig. 5. The evolutionof the velocityin the corotation zonesis indicatedby blue dotson both graphs.","AND LINDBLAD RESONANCES In the rotating reference frame around theZ–axis, the","AND LINDBLAD RESONANCES In the rotating reference frame around theZ–axis, the",,,,,Comparing dynamical effects of the central bar and the spiral arms in the solar neighborhood,"Willian Y. Nacafucasaco, Tatiana A. Michtchenko, Douglas Barros, Jacques Lépine",astro-ph.GA,2026-02-28T20:23:14Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00800v1,2603.00800,galactic_dynamics,98,75,False,False,700,775,False,1.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_000,0,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.MODEL AND SIMULATION In our previous work (Dengetal. 2024), we developeda realistic merger model for the MW. This model successfully reproduced the Galactic disk warp, capturing almost all its observed features. However, recent studies (Jiaoetal. 2023; Ouetal. 2023) suggest that the MW rotation curve declines more rapidlyat larger radii. Therefore, an improved modelis neededto accurately represent the mass distributionof the galaxy. The numerical initial conditions (ICs) were generated using the Disk Initial Conditions Environment (DICE) code (Perretetal. 2014; Perret 2016). Density profiles for DM, stars, and gas were input into DICE as distribution functions. These functions were then usedto generate Lagrangian particles via the Metropolis-Hastings Monte Carlo Markov Chain algorithm (Metropolisetal. 1953). The mass resolutionis 1×105M⊙for baryonic particles and DM particles, such resolutionis sufficientto track the warp structures, as the presenceof warp has also been observedin some lower resolution cosmological simulation suchas TNG100 (Semczuketal. 2020). The modeling approachin this study utilizes the GIZMO code (Hopkins 2015). We implementeda star formation and feedback module following Wangetal.",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_001,1,"the warp structures, as the presenceof warp has also been observedin some lower resolution cosmological simulation suchas TNG100 (Semczuketal. 2020). The modeling approachin this study utilizes the GIZMO code (Hopkins 2015). We implementeda star formation and feedback module following Wangetal. (2012), enabling gas particlesto convert into stellar particles and vice versa through feedback mechanisms. GIZMO isa variantof the smooth particle hydrodynamics code Gadget-3, employing Adaptive Gravity Softening (see detailsin Hopkinsetal. 2018). 2.1.GSE Our starting pointis basedon GSE mass measuredby Laneetal. (2023). Using high-purity samplesof APOGEE DR16 red giants selectedby kinematics and chemistry, they estimated the massof the GSE remnantas 1.45+0.92 −0.51×108M⊙, witha total mass∼6×1010M⊙. The GSE model consists ofa stellar disk, a gas disk, anda DM halo. Considering mass loss during the merger process, the total massof the GSE progenitor usedin the simulationis setto 7.2×1010M⊙, whichis 20% higher than the reference value (Leeetal. 2018). The stellar and gas disk models Table 1.Initial Conditions atz∼2 Progenitor Parameters Milky Way GSE Units Dark Matter mass 11.55 6.93 1×1010M⊙ α3.5 3.25 N/A Dark Matter scale radius 12.867 8.02 kpc Stellar disk mass 15",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,False,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_002,2,"reference value (Leeetal. 2018). The stellar and gas disk models Table 1.Initial Conditions atz∼2 Progenitor Parameters Milky Way GSE Units Dark Matter mass 11.55 6.93 1×1010M⊙ α3.5 3.25 N/A Dark Matter scale radius 12.867 8.02 kpc Stellar disk mass 15 0.2 1×109M⊙ Stellar scale length 2 0.6 kpc Stellar scale height 0.8 0.3 kpc Gas disk mass 30 2.5 1×109M⊙ Gas scale length 6 2.4 kpc Gas scale height 3 1.2 kpc Bulge mass 19.5 N/A 1×109M⊙ Bulge scale 0.9 N/A kpc Orbital Parameters Eccentricity (e) 0.9 Initial distance 100.5 kpc Inclination angle 15◦,30◦,45◦,55◦,60◦,75◦degree Relative velocity 73.02 km/s followan exponential + sech-z profile. The stellar massis setto the median reference valueof 2×108M⊙. The stellar disk scale lengthR ⋆(approximately 0.6 kpc), is determined using the size-mass relation (SMR) from Mowlaetal. (2019). The disk scale heightis 0.5×R ⋆, basedon simulations showing thatz∼2 disks become thick dueto turbulent gas and remain thick (Parketal. 2021). Given thatit isa gas-rich merger atz∼2, we assumea high gas fractionof 0.92 for the GSE. Following assumptions from galaxy merger and metallicity gradient simulationsby Coxetal. (2008) and Rochaetal. (2008),",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,280,460,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_003,3,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model In our previous study, we successfully reproduced the disk warp usinga gas-rich GSE merger model, in which the progenitor galaxy was modeled following Naiduetal. (2021). In that model, the DM halo was represented usinga Hernquist profile (Hernquist 1990) anda standard flattened distribution. In the current work, we adoptan Einasto profileto reproduce the declining rotation curve, which lead toa different gravitational potentialon the disk plane. Figure 11 shows comparisonof two different model’s evolutionof disk warp amplitude, which are both ona 55◦ inclination, clearly indicating that the flattened DM halo model producesa stronger warp. We mark the moments when each model reaches its maximum amplitude and the MW-like warp amplitude with dotsin the top panelof Figure 11. The bottom panels compare them= 1 mode acceleration amplitudeson the disk plane. The left panel shows the MW-like warp, withan amplitudeof approximately∼1 kpc atR = 16 kpc), where solid lines represent the DM acceleration withinR≤20 kpc and the DM mass for decline modelis",5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,True,True,0,180,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_004,4,"compare them= 1 mode acceleration amplitudeson the disk plane. The left panel shows the MW-like warp, withan amplitudeof approximately∼1 kpc atR = 16 kpc), where solid lines represent the DM acceleration withinR≤20 kpc and the DM mass for decline modelis about 1.3×1011M⊙, for the flatten modelis 1.9×1011M⊙, and dashed lines show the acceleration from 20 kpc≤R≤50 kpc, the DM massin this rangeis about 3.3×1010M⊙and 2.24×1011M⊙for decline and flatten model respectively. We observe that similar warp amplitudes can result from similar acceleration magnitudes, evenin different mass distribution models. In the declining DM model, mostof the massis concentratedin the inner region (R≤2.5 kpc), resultingin gravitational acceleration amplitudes that arean orderof magnitude higher than thoseof the flattened modelin the inner region. Specifically, the outer halo accelerationis oneto two ordersof magnitude weaker than that from the central region. The right panel shows the maximum warp amplitudes for each model: approximately∼5.4 kpc for the flattened model and∼2.7 kpc for the declining model. While the outer halo acceleration remains nearly unchanged, the central DM halo showsa significant increasein acceleration. The central acceleration amplitudein the flattened modelis",5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_005,5,"warp amplitudes for each model: approximately∼5.4 kpc for the flattened model and∼2.7 kpc for the declining model. While the outer halo acceleration remains nearly unchanged, the central DM halo showsa significant increasein acceleration. The central acceleration amplitudein the flattened modelis roughly twice thatof the declining model. As shownin Figure 11, although the models adopt different DM halo profiles, the resulting disk warps are drivenby similar gravitational strengths originating from the inner halo. An increasein accelerationon the disk plane correspondstoan increasein disk warp amplitude. Although the decline model may notbe sufficienton its ownto fully explain the evolutionof the MW warp during its later stages, its contribution should notbe overlooked. The model may still account for partof the present-day warp amplitude. Another scenariois proposedby Koopetal. (2024), who suggest that repeated perturbations from satellite passages have significantly disturbed the MW disk, rendering its rotation curve unreliable for mass estimation. This might imply that the halo profileis still flattenedin reality.",5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,5.3.Comparing with the flatten DM halo model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,203,156,False,False,280,436,True,5.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_006,6,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 4.2.Kinematic warp model In the previous subsection, we examined the evolutionof the Galactic warp, emphasizing that its rapid changesin amplitude reveal the inherently dynamical natureof warps. Understanding this dynamismis essential for tracing the formation historyof galaxies and determining the mass distributionof their halos. The changing geometryof the warp canbe characterizedby the variationin the directionof its linesof nodes (LONs), which precess ata rateω. This relationshipis given byϕ w(t) =ϕ w,0+ωtwhereϕ w,0denotes the current positionof the LONs. A time-dependent warp model basedon this frameworkis presentedby Poggioetal. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ, t= 0) = (Vϕ R−ω(R))hw(R)cos(ϕ−ϕ w) +∂hw ∂tsin(ϕ−ϕ w).(9) Herehw(R) =a(R−Rw)b, as describedin Eq. 1, VZrepresents the mean vertical velocity and Vϕrepresents the mean azimuthal velocity. To simplify the calculations, we neglect the time derivative term∂hw ∂tand minimize the lengthof theRbin and assume thatωremains constant with respect toR. Although the warpis evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effecton the precession results. Under these assumptions, the model canbe expressedin the same formas Equation 7 in Poggioetal.",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,0,180,True,4.5
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_007,7,"the lengthof theRbin and assume thatωremains constant with respect toR. Although the warpis evolving, our calculations show that this approximation has little effecton the precession results. Under these assumptions, the model canbe expressedin the same formas Equation 7 in Poggioetal. (2020): VZ(R, ϕ) = (Vϕ R−ω)hw(R)cos(ϕ−ϕ w).(10) Using this kinematic model, once the geometric shapeis determinedby fitting Eq.7, we can substitute the parameters hwandϕto measure the precession rateωbasedon the simulation’s kinematic data. For this analysis, we select young stars (age<1.5 Gyr) within the radial rangeR= 7−17 kpc. The regionis then divided into 20 radial binsto track and plot the temporal evolution ofω. Figure 5 shows time evolutionof stellar disk precession across different inclination groups. Long-term prograde precessionis more frequently observedin the high-inclination group, although transient high prograde precessionis also occasionally observedin the low-inclination group, it appears asa temporary feature andis generally dominatedby retrograde precession for the majorityof the simulation time. The disk warp canbe describedas anm = 1 bending wave propagating ina kinematically cool disk. Sucha wave canbe viewedas the superpositionof ‘fast’ (+) and ‘slow’ (−) waves circulating with frequenciesω=mΩ(R)±ν(R),",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,140,320,True,4.5
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_008,8,"so differential rotation windsitup rapidly andit decays. The ‘slow’ wave, on the other hand, is retrograde and circulates with frequency only weakly depending onRfor most radii. This waveis thus expectedto windup slowly andbe long-lived. In other words, prograde waves generally disappear quickly, except for persistent disturbances. In addition, accordingto Eq. 10, a positiveω(prograde precession) leads toa reductionin the vertical velocityof the disk. Conversely, retrograde precession increases the vertical velocity. Therefore, when the diskis ina high prograde but transient precession phase, the warp amplitude often reachesa minimum ina short timeof its evolution, anda low prograde but long-lasting precession, the warp amplitudeisin the processof continuous attenuation For example, these behaviors canbe seen during the periodsof 3−3.5 Gyr and 5−5.5 Gyrin the 15◦inclination, and during the periodof 3−11 Gyrof the 75◦inclinationin Figure 5 respectively.",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,171,131,False,False,420,551,True,4.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_009,9,"generally dominatedby retrograde precession for the majorityof the simulation time. The disk warp canbe describedas anm = 1 bending wave propagating ina kinematically cool disk. Sucha wave canbe viewedas the superpositionof ‘fast’ (+) and ‘slow’ (−) waves circulating with frequenciesω=mΩ(R)±ν(R), where Ω(R) is the angular rotation curve,ν(R) is the vertical frequency, andm-fold rotational symmetryis assumed. The inclusionof the disk’s selfgravity raisesν(R), making bending waves more stable, i.e. stiffer (contraryto density waves) – see Binney & Tremaine (2008). Fora given rotation curveω(R), bending waves can only propagatein regions that satisfy the condition [ΩP−Ω(R)]2≥ν2 h.(11) where Ω p=ω/mis the pattern speed andν his the frequencyof vertical oscillation contributedby the halo potential. This defines, form = 1, a ‘forbidden’ region, Ω−ν h<Ω p<Ω+ν h, where bending waves cannot propagate. Generally, ν >Ω, so the ‘fast’ waveis prograde, witha frequencyωdepending strongly onRfor most radii, so differential rotation windsitup rapidly andit decays. The ‘slow’ wave, on the other hand, is retrograde and circulates with frequency only weakly depending onRfor most radii. This waveis thus expectedto windup slowly andbe long-lived. In other words, prograde waves generally",4.2.Kinematic warp model,4.2.Kinematic warp model,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,True,280,460,True,3.5
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_010,10,"disks become thick dueto turbulent gas and remain thick (Parketal. 2021). Given thatit isa gas-rich merger atz∼2, we assumea high gas fractionof 0.92 for the GSE. Following assumptions from galaxy merger and metallicity gradient simulationsby Coxetal. (2008) and Rochaetal. (2008), the gas disk scale lengthis setto 4×R ⋆, witha scale height equalto halfof this length.",2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,2.MODEL AND SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,72,55,False,False,420,475,True,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_011,11,"A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger 2.2.Milky Way The MW massis determined basedon the hypothesis that the GSE merger wasa major merger witha mass ratioof 2.5: 1 (Belokurovetal. 2018; Naiduetal. 2021). Therefore, the virial massof the MW is settobe 1.8×1011M⊙. Additionally, we requirean appropriate representationof the MW during the merger epoch. Ages derived from various methods suggest that nearly allof the current high-α/thick disk and the bulge formed atz >1. Therefore, we model the MW atz∼2 asa combinationof the present-day thick disk, gas disk, and bulge. Xiangetal. (2025) studied the age-dependent structure and star formation rateof the MW disk using high-αstars with substantial orbital angular momentum and precise age determinations. Referringto Fig. 4 in their work, we set the stellar disk massat approximately 1.5×1010M⊙atz∼2. Using the size-mass relation (SMR), the disk scale length (R ⋆) is settobe 2 kpc. The disk thicknessis setto 0.4×R ⋆, basedon Fig. 3 in Xiangetal. (2025). The gas fractionof the MW progenitoris setat 0.667. The scale lengthof the gas diskis 3×R ⋆, witha thicknessof half scale",2.2.Milky Way,2.2.Milky Way,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,0,180,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_012,12,"scale length (R ⋆) is settobe 2 kpc. The disk thicknessis setto 0.4×R ⋆, basedon Fig. 3 in Xiangetal. (2025). The gas fractionof the MW progenitoris setat 0.667. The scale lengthof the gas diskis 3×R ⋆, witha thicknessof half scale length. Both disks followan exponential + sech-z profile. For the MW bulge atz∼2, we adopt the Bulge-E model from Jiaoetal. (2023), witha massof 1.95×1010M⊙anda scale length (Rb) of 0.9 kpc. The bulgeis modeled usingan Einasto profile (Einasto 1965), analogousto theS´ ersic profile (S´ ersic 1968) but applicableto three-dimensional mass density distributions (Coe 2010). This choiceis reasonable given that theS´ ersic profileis widely usedto fit galaxy surface densities. Combining the above components, the total massis setat 6.72×1010M⊙. This value aligns with the baryonic massof approximately 6×1010M⊙reportedby Jiaoetal. (2023) and Ouetal. (2023).",2.2.Milky Way,2.2.Milky Way,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,169,130,True,False,140,270,False,3.0
