astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_014,14,"DM halo, whichis measured with DM particles for each bins, as the radiusis divided into 100 bins with the same numberof particles from 0 tor vir, the dense polylines around∼0.1 r virexhibitsa highly oblate shape.Bottom middle panelis the tilt angle between DM halo’s short axis with the disk planein each radius bins, andit showsan increasing trend from the center outward.Bottom right panel shows hist profileof DM halo energy distribution withinr vir. for the 15◦inclination caseasan example, as differences between various inclination angle settings are negligible. Observational data from Jiaoetal. (2023) and Ouetal. (2023) are included for comparison, demonstrating good ∼15 kpc. Since the outer regionof the RC mainly dependson the DM profile, additional detailsof the DM halo are providedin Figure 2. The virial radius (R vir) of this MW modelis about 131.05 kpc, definedas the radius within which the average dark matter density equals 200 times the critical densityof the universe (ρ cr). This valueis slightly higher than the 121.03 and 123.80 kpc reportedby Jiaoetal. (2023) and Ouetal. (2023), respectively. The top-middle panelof Figure 2 shows the enclosed mass within",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_015,15,"analyze the DM haloby this method, dividing the radius into 100 bins, each containingan equal number (17191) of DM particles. The dense polylines are concentrated within 0.2 r vir, indicating that the inner DM halois more prolate, which correspondsto the formation ofa dark matter bar. Meanwhile, in the densest region, the halo exhibitsa highly oblate shape. The triaxiality varies only slightly with increasing radius. The tilt angleof each bin showsan increasing trend from the center outward. However the angles are highly consistent within 0.2 r vir, indicating that the dark matter halois compact. The non-zero triaxiality and the radial variationof the tilt angle confirm that the halois triaxial and misaligned with the disk. We also apply the same methodto measure the time evolutionof triaxiality and tilt angle under different inclination settings. We selected DM particles within 20 kpc, a region that contains the majorityof the galactic mass. In the top panelof Figure 3, we plot triaxiality over simulation time from 3 to 11 Gyr. The inclination angles are divided into two groups: high (i >45◦) and low (i≤45◦). In the",3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,3.DM HALO IN SIMULATION,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,700,880,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_016,16,"stars ensures the robustnessof our results while being more realistic with observations. Since the Galactic stellar and gas disks exhibit similar warp amplitudes, the stellar and gas disks within the same inclination group also show comparable evolutionary trendsin Figure 4. This suggests they are dominatedby the same evolutionary mechanismor influencedby the same gravitational potential. In high-inclination groups, the warp amplitudeis larger during the early stages, possibly dueto the higher vertical angular momentumof the merger orbit. Most notably, a regeneration mechanism appearsin the warp evolution. At times, the disk warp amplitude declines toa near-flat state, onlyto regenerate and rise again toa new peak. Since the simulation involves onlya single initial merger event, this persistent, quasi-periodic regeneration provides strong evidence that the warpis drivenby long-lived internal processes.",4.1.Evolutionof disk warp,4.1.Evolutionof disk warp,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,163,125,False,False,700,825,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_017,17,"our modelis the gravitational influenceof the DM halo. In this merger model, the dynamic coupling between the DM halo and the diskin the later evolutionary stages, resultingin reduced gravitational disturbance. This trendis evidentin Figure 4 and the right panelof Figure 9. During the evolution, the disk warp undergoes repeated cyclesof disappearance and regeneration. However, the peak amplitudeof each regeneration event gradually decreases over time, which agrees with the observed general decreasein warp amplitude with decreasing redshift (Vladimiretal. 2025). Since gravitational disturbance scales with mass, this low-mass model strugglesto produce high warp amplitudesin the later stages. As notedin Section 4.2, observed prograde precession tendsto reduce vertical velocity and thereby suppress warp amplitude. This suggests that the Galactic warp may have hada larger amplitudein the past. Therefore, an additional sourceof gravitational disturbance maybe requiredto fully reproduce the Galactic warp. Previous studies suggest that the Sagittarius (Sgr) dwarf galaxy and the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC) could also induce disk warp (Steleaetal. 2024). Some studies also report retrograde precessionin observations (Huangetal. 2024), or more complex precession patterns that vary between low prograde and retrograde",5.1.Comparing with the Galactic warp,5.1.Comparing with the Galactic warp,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,234,180,False,False,140,320,False,3.0
astrobench_15ca427d22,astrobench_15ca427d22_chunk_018,18,"disk plane, analyzedat the ”MW-like” epoch (left) and the ”Max Amplitude” epoch (right). The solid lines represent the acceleration contribution from the inner halo (R≤20 kpc), while the dashed lines represent the contribution from the outer halo (20≤R≤50 kpc). The flattened model producesa stronger maximum warp ∼5.4 kpc comparedto the Declining model∼2.7 kpc. Crucially, the decomposition reveals that the warpis predominantly drivenby the inner halo (R≤20 kpc, solid lines), where the accelerationis 1–2 ordersof magnitude stronger than that from the outer halo (dashed lines). drivenby baryonic particlesis generally less than one-tenthof that providedby dark matter particles, except for correlation between the baryonic acceleration and the evolutionof the warp amplitude. Consequently, we conclude that, unlike the dark matter halo, the external baryonic massis not the primary driverof the disk warp.",5.2.Comparing with the baryonic vertical acceleration,5.2.Comparing with the baryonic vertical acceleration,,,,,A Low-mass Model of The Milky Way: The Disk Warp Resulting from A Galaxy Merger,"Mingji Deng, Cuihua Du, Jian Zhang, Haoyang Liu, Zhongbcheng Li",astro-ph.GA,2026-01-29T12:41:33Z,http://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21638v1,2601.21638,galactic_dynamics,168,129,False,False,420,549,False,3.0
